### TASK-260830-z1yxg9 — RPC envelope/hello + Host Channel 1.0.0 implemented
- DELIVERED: `internal/rpcwire` completed for RPC 5.0.0 (exact RPC-4 shapes, contracts.rpc [5.0.0], Error statically 1.3.0 via a new axerror binding row) and `internal/hostchannel` built: mutual TLS 1.3 (exact ax-host/1, enrolled roots only, no tickets/cache/resumption), VerifyPeer supplement (ALPN presence, no-resume, verified chain, unique exact leaf/root/key match, profile, trust state), authorized hello both roles via hosttrust.AuthorizeDispatch, current-generation dispatch/mutation boundaries, one framed Error 1.3.0 refusal then close, silent close for unframeable input and foreign majors. Preserved pre-credentials packet restored verbatim (sha256-verified); UNRESOLVED_QUESTIONS dropped as superseded by the v0.6.0 11.10 binding.
- F1: TLS 1.3 clients report handshake success before the server validates their flight, so rogue-cert refusal tests assert on the follow-up read, not the handshake call; the rogue must read (not write) first or the test deadlocks on the rendezvous pipe while the server blocks sending its alert. Production SSH pipes are OS-buffered and immune; the deadlock is a net.Pipe harness artifact.
- F2: Go wraps received TLS alerts as `*tls.permanentError` (unexported, implements net.Error with Timeout()=false) unwrapping to `*net.OpError{Op:"remote error"}`. The classifier maps Op=="remote error" to authentication_failed and every other OpError/timeout/EOF variant to transport_failure; matching on net.Error alone misclassifies alerts as transport.
- F3: `fmt.Errorf("%w: %v", sentinel, err)` formats the cause into the message WITHOUT chaining it; all 37 channel wraps now use `%w: %w` so errors.Is/As see both the refusal identity and the hosttrust cause. This one-character class of bug hid the identity-mismatch chain end to end.
- F4: Go caps handshake messages at 256 KiB, so the specified 1 MiB handshake byte cap cannot fire through a real TLS stack; it is unit-proven at the exact boundary (mutant-killed) and integration-proven as bounded intake (4 MB hostile preface reads < 64 KiB and fails fast). Unframeable reads now close the stream (found by the oversize-line test hanging on the half-open pipe).
- MUTATION EVIDENCE: hostchannel 1 neutral passed + 22 narrowing killed (incl. hello-ID substitution, stale-dispatch/mutation, cap+1, CA-bound+1, tickets, both TLS-min roles) + 1 documented harmless SURVIVED (client session cache without server tickets); token-preserving census-evasion mutant passes the static census and is killed by the runtime census with the full suite running. rpcwire 1 neutral passed + 34 narrowing killed (incl. new v5-exact). Per-probe overlays/logs/tables under /tmp/hcmut and /tmp/rwmut; packaged into the task outcome tar.
- VALIDATION: hostchannel + rpcwire green incl. -race; cover hostchannel 81.6/rpcwire 97.8; fuzz gate added to task-board.config.json and passing; axerror binding row + pinned tests updated; traceability gaps for 11.2/11.3/11.8/11.9/11.10/15.1/15.3 rewritten (coverage levels unchanged, no new clause discharge claimed) with reviewed digest 5102d3bce23e6f27264232332a300dbb89237dc7792673efe9ec039b1219ce08; README figures pin green.
- AC: 7 of 7 RPC rows driven through Serve/Dial/Call by named tests (see hostchannel README table). Stated bounds: no ax CLI/SSH launch/provider plugin (computed Config-4 argv only), SSH-carrier parity by construction (no SSH-impl branch), async one-second close via WatchGeneration polling, TLS alert text owned by crypto/tls. Candidate UNCOMMITTED in the Story worktree; no review acceptance or main integration claimed.

