### 1447 — The exit status a document declares is a member, not a fact: a guard proven on one code let a relabelled envelope forge a retry claim

- SCOPE: new `internal/axerror/agreement_test.go` and `internal/cliresult/relabel_test.go`, `README.md`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` and its projection digest. Round-6 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §14.2, §15.1, §15.2, §15.3. No production code changed — every round-6 finding was an evidence defect, not a behaviour defect.
- ROOT CAUSE: the code-to-exit-status agreement in `axerror.decodeBody`, `if exitCode != expectedExit`, was covered by exactly one row of `TestDecodeRefusesClosedShapeViolations`: `observation_gap` driven at exit 5 instead of its own exit 9. One code, one wrong status, out of 752 ordered (code, wrong-status) pairs at 1.0.0 and 1,744 at 1.3.0. Narrowing the guard to `code == "observation_gap"` — restricting it to exactly the pair that row drives — left `go test ./... -count=1` at exit 0 across all 13 packages; so did sparing `policy_refused` or `authentication_failed` individually. Only deleting the guard reddened anything. Reproduced SURVIVED at the reviewed head before a line of test code was written.
- FINDING: this was a reachable bypass and not only an unmeasured ratio. `code` and `exit_code` are two independent members of one document, and this guard is the sole thing binding them. The retryability refusal this package publishes keys on the EXIT STATUS for three whole classes — 7 authorization, 16 policy refusal, 130 interrupt — so relabelling a code out of its own class asks that refusal about the wrong class and it stays silent. Under the narrowing, `{"code":"authentication_failed","exit_code":9,"retryable":true}` was ADMITTED by `cliresult.Read` at exit status 9, and the caller received a `Reading` naming an authorization failure with `Retryable() == true`. The §14.2 exit-status corroboration cannot see it, because the process really did exit with the relabelled status.
- FINDING: this is the FOURTH guard on this leaf published proven at one sample, after both `ErrOutcomeDisagreement` guards in entry 1231 and the command-tag agreement in entry 1421. Each round closed the guards it was shown and did not sweep the sibling on the same reading path. The generalisation, stated once: when a class of "proven at one sample" is found, the remedy is to enumerate every guard on that path and measure each over its own domain, not to fix the one that was named.
- FIX: `TestTheCodeToExitStatusAgreementIsMeasuredOverTheWholeCodeRegistry` drives all 316 registered codes across the 4 registered versions at all 17 registered failure statuses — 5,372 rows. The agreeing status must be ADMITTED and each of the other sixteen refused with this guard's own sentence naming both numbers, so a pair settled by `decodeExitStatus`, by the closed member set, or by the retryability gate does not count as coverage of it. Per-version totals are pinned at 752, 1,056, 1,504 and 1,744 and re-derived from `CodesFor` and `CodesByExitStatus`.
- DECISION: close the bypass at the production entry point as well as at the decoder, because they are different claims. `TestRelabellingACodeOutOfItsExitClassCannotForgeARetryClaimThroughRead` takes the 7 codes of Structured Error 1.0.0 whose own class carries an exit-keyed retryability refusal, relabels each into all 14 statuses that carry none, and requires `Read` to refuse all 98. The control in the other direction is required in the same loop — the same code at its own status with `retryable: true` must be refused by the retryability gate — which is what makes the relabelling a bypass of something rather than a rewrite of nothing.
- FINDING (reviewer observation, closed in the same pass): the registered-status admission in `decodeExitStatus` was driven at `{0, 1, 18, 99}`, so `!IsFailureExitStatus(status) && status != 42` survived the whole suite. The complement of a four-value sample is not a bound. `TestTheExitStatusAdmissionIsSweptOverEveryByteValue` sweeps 0..255 plus six out-of-range values and requires admission if and only if the §15.2 table registers the status as a failure.
- DECISION: an oracle must not be the predicate under test. The sweep above enumerates the expected admissions through `ExitStatusMeaning`, not `IsFailureExitStatus`, because the latter IS the decision being measured and an oracle built from it would agree with whatever the mutant narrowed it to. The asserted row count of seventeen is what catches a mutation that moves both. The sweep also drives a code the registry does not carry, which takes the §15.3 unknown-code branch and skips the agreement guard, so exactly one decision is left in the row.
- ANOMALY: the two guards closed here are on the same function as gates this leaf has swept three times, and neither appeared in any of the 14-, 23-, 28-, 44- or 53-mutant harnesses. The harness grew by whatever the last review named. A hand-picked mutant set measures the reviewer's attention, not the code — the same shape entry 2113 records for a sweep that selects its own mutants.
- BOUND, unchanged and restated: `tracecheck` still reports `acceptance_cases=74` and `clauses_discharged=17/403`. All three new tests register against existing acceptance cases rather than new ones, so no coverage figure moved; each registration was probed by renaming its declaration and confirming `tracecheck` names the missing declaration ahead of the projection digest.
- EVIDENCE: harness grown from 53 to 63 mutants — 62 killed, 1 declared subsumed (unchanged), 0 unexplained survivors, 44 narrowing by the harness's own definition, suite restored green. Supersedes the "53 mutants at 52 killed" figure in the 1421 entry below. Same stated bound: the harness mutates the tree, lives under `.temp/`, and no committed artifact derives its counts.
