### 1421 — A guard proven at one ordered pair admits a forged authority document, and the same blind spot sits in the sibling reader

- SCOPE: `internal/cliresult/client_test.go` (one subtest rewritten into a both-directions sweep, one new grid test), `internal/cliresult/datamodel_test.go` and `internal/axerror/datamodel_test.go` (large-document rows), `README.md`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` and its projection digest. Round-5 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §14.2, §1.6. No production code changed — every round-5 finding was an evidence defect, not a behaviour defect.
- ROOT CAUSE: the command-tag agreement in `readSuccess`, `if result.Command() != command`, was covered by exactly one row: invoked `doctor`, document reporting `list`. Narrowing it to `result.Command() != CommandTakeover` — admit any document CLAIMING to be a takeover result — left the whole repository suite at exit 0, all 13 packages `ok`. With that in place `Read(CommandList, <a document carrying "command": "takeover">)` hands the caller a `Reading` whose `Result()` is a takeover body, the one body in this contract carrying adoption and authority semantics, for an invocation that never ran it. Reproduced SURVIVED at the reviewed head before a line of test code was written; a control sparing `CommandDoctor` was KILLED in the same run, so the one existing row was real and was all there was.
- FINDING: this is the THIRD guard on this leaf published proven at one sample, after both `ErrOutcomeDisagreement` guards in entry 1231. The two neighbouring subtests in the SAME test function already sweep their whole domain and assert the sweep count. The pattern was in the file, two screens up, and was not applied to the row between them.
- FIX: the agreement is driven over the whole implemented vocabulary in both directions — every implemented tag as the invocation against every other implemented tag's own emitted document — and each refusal must carry this guard's own sentence naming both tags, so a pair settled by the version binding does not count as coverage of it. The 18 agreeing pairs are required to be ADMITTED in the same loop, which is what stops a guard that refuses everything from passing the sweep.
- DECISION: state the guard's share of the vocabulary as a ratio with its denominator rather than as a bound in prose. `TestTheCommandAgreementGuardOwnsAMeasuredShareOfTheTagVocabulary` drives all 1,936 ordered pairs over the 44 registered tags and classifies every answer: 18 admitted, 306 refused by this guard, 1,144 refused before stdout is read because the invoked tag selects a version this repository does not build, 468 refused by the version binding because the document claims a tag bound to another major. "The guard is never reached for a pair involving an unimplemented tag" is then a measured fact with a named earlier refusal behind it, not an assumption that nothing was bypassed.
- FINDING (reviewer Observation A, extended and acted on rather than recorded as a bound): the reviewer measured that narrowing `axerror.requireCommonDataModel` to skip documents over 4096 bytes SURVIVES, because every duplicate-member fixture is hand-built and short, and declined to block because byte length is not a §1.6 dimension. The identical narrowing on `documentSchema`'s copy of the same gate in `cliresult` also survived — the finding was reported for one reader and holds in both. Both are closed with a conforming document several times that size: a Structured Error padded through its 64-key `details` bound, and a CLI Result carrying twelve Session Summaries.
- DECISION: give a size fixture ONE knob. The first padding helper padded through both the 4096-character `message` bound and the `details` map, so narrowing either loop alone left the document over 4096 and the mutant survived as an equivalent one. Padding through `details` alone makes the narrowed loop redden the row's own length assertion. A fixture whose size has two independent sources cannot be shown to fail closed.
- ANOMALY: `Read` refuses the forged-tag pairs from three different places depending on which side is unimplemented, and only one of them is this guard. Counting refusals rather than classifying them would have reported 1,918 refusals and 306 of them would have been invisible; the classification is what turns "everything is refused" into "this guard refuses exactly these".
