### 1338 — A figure published beside a measurement must be derived from it, not restated next to it: third instance in one artifact

- SCOPE: `README.md` (one figure corrected), new `internal/traceability/readme_figures_pin_test.go`, `internal/cliresult/client_test.go` (two new sweeps), `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` and its projection digest. Round-4 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §14.2, §15.1, §15.2. No production code changed — every round-4 finding was an evidence defect, not a behaviour defect.
- ROOT CAUSE: `README.md` published "73 executable acceptance cases" while `ownership.v0.5.0.json` held 74 entries with 74 distinct ids and `tracecheck` printed `acceptance_cases=74`. The first pass of this leaf moved the registry from 65 to 73 and updated the sentence; the round-2 rework added a case and did not. Nothing measured the figure — setting it to 999 left `go test ./...` and `tracecheck` both at exit 0.
- FINDING: this is the THIRD occurrence of the identical class inside this one artifact, and the first two are recorded here by the same author. Entry 1015: the "nineteen §15.2 rows" claim against an eighteen-row table. Entry 1157: two of four published fan-in rows wrong, root cause named at the time as "prose nobody measured". The response to the second was `TestREADMEFanInTableIsDerivedFromTheMeasuredProjection`, which parses the README and re-derives every cell — and it sits two hundred lines from a sentence that restates instead of derives and is off by one. The tool for the class existed, in the repository, written for this exact failure, and was never pointed at the neighbouring paragraph.
- DECISION: the general rule, worth stating once. A figure published beside a measurement must be an INPUT to the test, not a second copy of the answer. Three instances in one artifact is not carelessness; it is the natural failure mode of prose sitting near data. The only fix that holds is parsing the prose and re-deriving every number in it from the thing it describes.
- FIX: `TestREADMEOwnershipFiguresAreDerivedFromTheMeasuredReport` locates the paragraph by its heading, refuses a heading it finds twice or not at all, and re-derives all seven figures from `traceability.VerifyRepository` — not only the one that drifted, because the other six were correct today and unmeasured for exactly the same reason. Each figure is located by the noun phrase naming what it counts, so re-ordering the clause cannot re-point a pin at a different number.
- DECISION: the pin polices its own completeness. Every digit run in the paragraph that no pinned phrase consumed is reported as unmeasured, with only dotted version and path tokens exempt under a deliberately narrow rule. That makes DELETING a row from the pinned-figure list a red rather than a quietly smaller comparison — the failure mode entry 2113 records for a derived gate that did not police its own extraction pattern.
- FINDING (reviewer Observation A, acted on): `exitStatusIsNotEnough` documents its own count as "a measured count rather than as advice" and `README.md` repeated the claim, but every assertion on those refusals matched the trailing clause `identifies no failure` and nothing read the number. Replacing `len(fanIn[exitStatus])` with a constant, or the whole `%d registered` clause with the word "many", left the suite green. Both are killed now, and so is the narrowing that keeps the count correct at exit 6 only — which is exactly the single assertion proposed as the minimal fix. The sweep drives every registered command at every registered §15.2 failure status through `Read` and re-derives each answer from `CodesByExitStatus`.
- FINDING (reviewer Observation B, acted on): retry independence from the exit status was proven at exit 12 alone, and the one exit-15 envelope any test drove already declared `retryable: true`, so a fabricated "if the status is 15, retry" branch returned the answer the document would have given and survived. `TestTheRetryDecisionFollowsTheDocumentAtEveryFailureStatus` drives both polarities across the whole failure domain; the true arm covers fourteen of seventeen statuses because the pinned document forbids the claim for every code of the other three, and that figure is pinned rather than left implicit.
- DECISION (reviewer Observation C, left alone): the two survivors the round-3 reviewer checked as genuinely equivalent — `Succeeded()` recomputed from `exitStatus`, and `ExitStatus()` returned from `failure.ExitCode()` — are NOT "fixed". They survive because `readSuccess` and `readFailure` between them make document and status provably agree on every admitted reading. A survivor dismissed with a reason is evidence; one quietly fixed hides why it survived. The same holds for the two round-2 dismissals, the near-equivalent `details` narrowing of `requireCommonDataModel` and the `CodesByExitStatus` empty-group pre-seed.
- ANOMALY: my first hand-probe of the "many" mutant reported a non-zero exit and I read it as a kill. It was a BUILD failure — dropping the `%d` argument left `fanIn` declared and not used. The harness's `NOT-COMPILED` arm caught it on the next run. A mutant that does not compile proves nothing about the test, and a non-zero exit status is not by itself a kill.
- EVIDENCE: harness grown from 28 to 44 mutants — 43 killed, 1 declared subsumed (unchanged), 0 unexplained survivors, suite restored green. Supersedes the "28 mutants at 27 killed" figure in the 1231 entry below.
- DECISION: the "narrows rather than deletes" figure is no longer counted by hand either. The class is now DEFINED in the harness — enforcement left in place for part of the domain, whether a restricted class, one of several redundant copies, a reordering, or a published claim kept and given a specific wrong value — and the count computed from that definition, with the harness failing closed if the set names a mutant that does not exist. Applied to the 28-mutant harness the definition selects exactly the fourteen that revision published; the round-4 count is twenty-seven. Same lesson as the entry title, applied to my own evidence prose rather than to somebody else's.
- BOUND, stated rather than glossed: the harness mutates the working tree, so it lives under `.temp/` and is attached to the board item. `go test ./...` does not run it and no committed artifact derives its counts. `README.md` now says so where it publishes them, instead of letting a harness figure sit beside gated ones and read as though it were gated too.

### 1231 — A gate proven at one sample is a gate proven at one sample: both §14.2 outcome guards shipped with a narrowing the suite could not see

- SCOPE: `internal/cliresult/client_test.go` (two new sweeps, one new test), README mutation prose, `internal/traceability` ownership registration. Round-3 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §14.2, §15.2, §15.3. No production code changed — the implementation was correct in both findings and was not asked to move.
- ROOT CAUSE: this leaf published the standard "a gate whose only evidence is that deleting it reddens something has been shown to exist, not to hold" and then failed it on its own two `ErrOutcomeDisagreement` guards. Each was driven at exactly one row, so each admitted a narrowing that the whole suite could not distinguish from the real guard.
- FINDING (F1): `if failure.CodeRegistered() && failure.ExitCode() != output.ExitStatus` survived `go test ./internal/cliresult ./internal/axerror`. The uncovered row is precisely where the guard is the SOLE enforcement: `axerror.decodeBody` cross-checks `exit_code` against the pinned registry only when `ExitCodeFor` resolves, and for a code a later compatible minor added it takes the `ErrUnregisteredCode` branch and runs no exit check at all. For a registered code the equality is bound twice; for an unregistered one, `readFailure` is all there is. Reproduced with the frozen `error-1.0.0-later-minor-code.json`: a machine client is handed exit class 5, "workspace/native-store conflict; no silent overwrite", for a document declaring exit class 10, ownership/lease/fencing — two different remediations, chosen from the wrong one.
- FINDING: the insight had already been had once on this leaf and not carried across. `error-1.0.0-later-minor-code-forged-retry.json` exists because an unregistered code must not be a way past the §15.3 RETRY rule. The identical reasoning applies to the EXIT-CLASS rule and was never applied to it.
- FINDING (F2): `if output.ExitStatus != SuccessExitStatus && output.ExitStatus != 130` survived the same suites, admitting a CLI Result success object at exit 130 as `Succeeded() == true`. Exit 130 is the §15.2 row "interrupted by operator signal before a clean response", which makes it the one failure status at which a success document plausibly does reach stdout before the process dies — so a narrowing at exactly that status is a realistic bypass, not an artificial one.
- FIX: both rows now sweep every registered §15.2 failure status rather than one sample, drawn from the production predicate `axerror.IsFailureExitStatus` through one helper that asserts the count is seventeen, so a predicate that answered false for everything would redden instead of turning each loop into a green test that drove nothing. `TestTheExitStatusEqualityBindsACodeTheRegistryDoesNotCarry` first asserts `CodeRegistered() == false` on the conforming read, so the row cannot quietly stop covering the class it exists for if that code is ever registered.
- DECISION: the enumerator itself carries a mutant. Narrowing its range to `status <= 20` drops exit 130 out of every loop that draws on it, and the asserted count is what makes that a red rather than a silently smaller sweep. A shared helper that decides how much a sweep covers is a gate, and it is measured like one.
- EVIDENCE: harness grown from 23 to 28 mutants — 27 killed, 1 declared subsumed (unchanged), 0 unexplained survivors, each mutant verified applied, compiled, and carrying the MUTATED text on disk before measurement, suite restored green. Fourteen now narrow rather than delete. Both round-2 survivors were reproduced as SURVIVED on the unchanged tree first and are KILLED after; supersedes the "23 mutants at 22 killed" figure in the 1157 entry below.
- EVIDENCE: the new test is registered in the `cli-result-machine-reading` acceptance case and the ownership projection digest re-pinned. That registration is not decorative and was not assumed to be checked: renaming the declaration to one that does not exist makes `tracecheck` fail with `declaration ... is absent from "internal/cliresult/client_test.go"` — the declaration-resolution check answers before the digest check does.
- EVIDENCE: `go build`, `go vet`, `gofmt -l ./internal` clean; `go test ./... -count=1` exit 0 across 13 packages; `-cover` exit 0 with `cliresult` 95.5% and `axerror` 99.5% (both unchanged — the sweeps add rows through statements already executed); `tracecheck` exit 0 at `acceptance_cases` 74 and `clauses_discharged` 17/403, both unchanged, because a test added to an existing acceptance case discharges no new clause.
- NOTE: the two survivors the reviewer checked and dismissed were left alone as instructed — the `details`-bearing narrowing of `requireCommonDataModel` is near-equivalent on conforming input, and pre-seeding an empty group per failure status in `CodesByExitStatus` is genuinely equivalent against today's registry. A survivor dismissed with a reason is evidence; a survivor quietly fixed hides why it survived.
- STATUS: Ready for review. TASK-260830-33sfxc round-3 rework, leaf 3 of 3 on `task-board/story/STORY-260830-2jylym`.

### 1157 — Two conforming readers, one document: a repeated member is not a parse curiosity, it is a forged retry claim from a remote peer

- SCOPE: `internal/axerror` (new `requireCommonDataModel`), `internal/cliresult` (`documentSchema` gate, `Read` refusal table), README/LOGBOOK fan-in figures. Rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §1.6, §15.1, §14.2.
- ROOT CAUSE: `encoding/json` does not refuse a document whose members repeat. It *resolves* the repeat, and it resolves it **differently per decode target**: a scalar member into a struct or a map keeps the last occurrence, and a map-typed member such as `details` MERGES both occurrences. `cliresult.Decode` ran `canonicaljson.Canonicalize`, whose strict decoder refuses duplicates. `axerror.Decode` referenced `canonicaljson` nowhere, so the FAILURE branch of the same reading did not.
- FINDING: the machine answers changed, not just the parse. Through `Read` on the frozen workspace-conflict fixture at its own exit status 5: a repeated `code` inside the same exit class was **admitted** and answered with the second occurrence; a repeated `retryable` flipped `Retryable()` from false to true — **a forged retry claim assembled from two members no conforming writer could emit**, on a code whose retryability refusal table was never consulted for it; a repeated `details` resolved to the **union** of both occurrences, which is neither one. The exit-status corroboration this leaf added cannot see any of it, because both occurrences share the exit class.
- FINDING: the reach is remote. `axerror.Decode` is also the reader for peer-supplied provider, task-board bridge, Mesh RPC, session-adapter and terminal-backend envelopes through `DecodeBound`, so the shape arrived from a peer and not only from a local pipe. `TestABoundPeerCannotSendADuplicateKeyedEnvelope` drives all fifteen bound contracts.
- ROOT CAUSE (the review-visible one): `documentSchema` justified not settling duplicates itself with "the closed decoder for the selected contract then validates the whole object, including the duplicate members". That sentence was true of one branch and false of the other, and it was **written in the same change that shipped the gap**. A delegation claim is a claim about the delegate; this one named no delegate and was never checked against either.
- DECISION: the gate goes on `axerror.Decode`, so one place covers every peer surface, and a second copy runs in `documentSchema` before it reads `schema` — because a repeated `schema` selects the branch, and neither closed decoder can settle that for the discriminator: both run after the branch is chosen. `TestADuplicateSchemaMemberCannotSelectTheBranch` pins it; without the discriminator's copy the reading answered `ErrOutcomeDisagreement`, a fact about the exit status, for a document whose schema it never settled.
- DECISION: **the canonical bytes are discarded, not adopted.** RFC 8785 §3.2.2.3 serializes numbers through `Number.prototype.toString`, so the transform rewrites `1e1` to `10` and `9.0` to `9`, and `decodeExitStatus` reads `exit_code` from its raw bytes precisely so those forms are refused. A gate that adopted its own output would be a **widening** dressed as validation. `TestTheCanonicalGateDoesNotLaunderTheExitStatusToken` proves both halves — that the transform really does rewrite the literal, and that `Decode` still refuses it — so the row cannot pass on a token nothing would have changed.
- DECISION: the gate runs **before** the envelope identity, extending the precedence this leaf already settled. A document whose `schema_version` repeats has no version, and an identity-first order would answer `ErrUnsupportedMajor` or `ErrVersionMismatch` from one of two members it had no basis for choosing between. Both orders refuse; only one declines to invent a version fact. `TestADuplicateIdentityMemberIsNotResolvedIntoAVersionFact` pins it and its reorder mutant is killed.
- FINDING: removing the gate reddens duplicates, malformed UTF-8, lone surrogate escapes and trailing content, and **does not** redden an unescaped control character — `validateMessage` refuses that byte independently. Measured against the delete mutant rather than assumed, and stated as a subsumed case instead of counted as coverage the gate provides. `parseEnvelopeIdentity`'s trailing-content guard was removed rather than left as a branch no test can redden, and the trailing rows prove the coverage moved with it.
- FINDING (F2): the published fan-in table was wrong in **two of its four rows** — `1.2.0` said 12 codes at exit 6 against a measured 14 at exit **16**, wrong count and wrong status; `1.3.0` said 15 against a measured 17 — and the error had been copied into entry 1003 below. Cause: `largest`/`largestFor` were asserted for `1.0.0` and `1.1.0` only, so the other two rows were prose nobody measured. Same class as the "nineteen §15.2 rows" defect the 1015 entry records, one artifact over.
- FIX: `TestREADMEFanInTableIsDerivedFromTheMeasuredProjection` **parses the README table** and re-derives every cell from `CodesByExitStatus`, requires every registered version to appear exactly once, refuses a version whose maximum ties across two statuses (the published row names one status and would otherwise be ambiguous), and a sibling test does the same for the two figures entry 1003 states in prose. Mutants that restore either wrong figure, or delete a row, are killed.
- FINDING (F3): `TestReadDistinguishesAbsenceFromAReadFailure` asserted `errors.Is` membership only, so aliasing `ErrAbsentDocument` onto `ErrUnreadableDocument` survived the suite. The first mutual-exclusion fix **also survived**: it skipped "the sentinel this row wanted" by comparing VALUES, and under the alias the two values are equal, so it skipped exactly the comparison that would have caught it. The rows now name their sentinel as a string. A negative assertion written in terms of the thing it is testing for is not a negative assertion.
- FINDING: the first draft of this fix cited Section 1.6 as fixing the wire format as "UTF-8 JSON restricted to the common logical data model". **That sentence is nowhere in the pinned document** — a paraphrase wearing quotation marks, caught by checking the citation through `internal/specdoc` instead of trusting the wording. The two real fragments, `SPEC.md:218` "map keys MUST be UTF-8 strings and MUST be unique" and `SPEC.md:221` "floating-point numbers, NaN, Infinity, non-string map keys, and duplicate keys are forbidden", are now located BY CONTENT and their section is re-derived, so the citation survives re-pagination; the absence of the paraphrase is asserted too, so the correction cannot be undone by someone who remembers the wording. Same class as the 0559 false-citation entry, one contract over.
- EVIDENCE: 23 mutants, each verified applied, compiled, and carrying the MUTATED text on disk before measurement; 22 killed, 1 declared subsumed, 0 unexplained survivors. Nine narrow rather than delete. `go build`, `go vet`, `gofmt -l` clean; `go test ./... -count=1` exit 0 across 13 packages; `-cover` exit 0 with `cliresult` 95.5% and `axerror` 99.5%; `tracecheck` exit 0 at `acceptance_cases` 73 → 74 and `clauses_discharged` 17/403 unchanged — the new acceptance case is attached to the §15.1 and §17.2 bindings as evidence and claims no additional clause, because the gate discharges no obligation the clause scanner measures.
- STATUS: Ready for review. TASK-260830-33sfxc rework, leaf 3 of 3 on `task-board/story/STORY-260830-2jylym`.

### 1003 — An exit status is a class, not an identity: the fan-in is 47 codes over 17 statuses, and no CLI process exists to produce either

- SCOPE: `internal/cliresult` (new `Read`, reordered `Decode`), `internal/axerror` (new `CodesByExitStatus`, new `parseEnvelopeIdentity`), two frozen historical envelope corpora (TASK-260830-33sfxc), §14.2/§15/§17.2.
- DECISION: the compatibility claim needed a **consuming** entry point, because until now nothing in the tree read a completed `ax` invocation - `Emit` writes one and returns a status, and the story's whole claim is about what a machine client may depend on. `Read(command, InvocationOutput{Stdout, ExitStatus})` is that call site: the document on stdout decides the outcome and the exit status only corroborates it.
- DECISION: `InvocationOutput` has exactly two members and **no stderr member**, so a reading structurally cannot depend on a diagnostic stream. A behavioural test can only show that today's code ignores a field it has; `TestMachineReadingCannotSeeStderr` asserts the member set through reflection and reddens the moment one appears. Its mutant adds `Stderr []byte` and is killed.
- FINDING: the exit status under-determines the failure by a **measured** ratio, not by argument. Structured Error 1.0.0 assigns 47 codes to 17 failure statuses, 14 of which carry more than one code, the largest being 6 codes at exit 6; 1.3.0 reaches 109 codes with 17 codes at exit 6. `CodesByExitStatus` is the production projection and the refusal messages quote its count, so a caller that logs `ErrAbsentDocument` is told *how many* codes the status leaves open instead of being advised to look elsewhere.
- FINDING: the ratio alone is not the decision a client makes with it. Under the `session.clone.*` binding (Error 1.1.0), exit 12 carries both `staging_incomplete`, which may claim `retryable`, and `transaction_unknown`, which §15.1 calls "a parked ambiguous effect, never success or absence" and which may not. A client keying on exit 12 would retry the parked one. `TestExitStatusAloneDecidesNoRetry` drives all three arms through `Read`.
- DECISION: absence, read failure, and foreign schema stay **three** refusals. `ErrAbsentDocument`, `ErrUnreadableDocument`, `ErrForeignDocument` - a truncated pipe is not a silent command, and neither is ever resolved from the status. The mutant that collapses absence into read failure is killed.
- DECISION: `session.clone.*` failures are readable here and their successes are not, and that asymmetry is the point. This repository builds no clone body, so `Read(CommandCloneRun, success)` returns `ErrUnimplementedVersion`; its failure is Error 1.1.0 and classifies completely. "This build cannot construct that success" and "this failure is unreadable" are different facts.
- EVIDENCE: two frozen corpora - 9 CLI invocations under `internal/cliresult/testdata/historical`, 7 protocol-bound envelopes under `internal/axerror/testdata/historical` covering all four bound error versions - each file pinned by SHA-256 with its expected machine answers recorded per row. Regenerating a fixture reddens the digest gate rather than the assertion, which is the whole reason the bytes are checked in instead of built: an envelope produced by today's constructor only shows the package agreeing with itself.
- EVIDENCE: message-independence is proved by replay, not by inspection. Every readable envelope is re-read with the message replaced - by another code's name, by a JSON object, by 4,096 characters, by non-ASCII - and every machine answer must be identical while `HumanMessage` must change. A row whose rewrite produced identical bytes fails rather than passing vacuously.
- FINDING: one mutant is **declared subsumed**: removing the exit-0 guard in `readFailure` leaves the suite green because `decodeExitStatus` never admits a Structured Error carrying `exit_code` 0, so the equality check refuses the same input one guard later. 14 mutants, each verified applied and compiled before measurement, 13 killed, 1 subsumed, 0 unexplained survivors. Superseded by the 1157 entry above: the harness is now 23 mutants at 22 killed / 1 subsumed, and the two additional survivors that appeared when the data-model gate landed were closed rather than declared.
- DECISION (the precedence question leaf 2's review deferred): **the envelope identity is settled before the closed member set, in both readers.** A document of an unbound major carrying an unknown top-level member used to be refused with the structural fact - `unknown top-level member "new_in_v2"` in `cliresult`, `unknown field` in `axerror` - and `errors.Is(err, ErrUnsupportedMajor)` was false. Both orders refuse, so nothing was ever admitted either way; what differed is which fact the caller is told, and a caller deciding "is my ax too old for this output" cannot decide it from a member name. The pinned text scopes the member rule to the object it governs three times - §1.6 "a reader MUST reject an unknown top-level field in **a major version 1 object**", §17.1 "**within any negotiated major version** ... unknown top-level fields remain an error", §17.2 rule 1 listed first - and §15.1 states the posture outright: "receivers MUST NOT parse a different major's payload far enough to trust its error code, retryable bit, details, or authority fields". `cliresult.decodeClosedDocument` split into `parseDocument` + `verifyClosedMembers`; `axerror` gained `parseEnvelopeIdentity` reading only `schema`/`schema_version` from the raw bytes.
- EVIDENCE: `TestUnsupportedMajorIsSettledBeforeTheClosedMemberRule` pins every row in both packages - six in `cliresult`, five in `axerror` - `TestReorderingTheIdentityCheckAdmitsNothingItUsedToRefuse` re-drives every shape the old order refused (13 rows in `cliresult`, 13 in `axerror`), and two harness mutants restore the previous order and are killed. `cliresult.rawString` now separates "the member is absent" from "the member is not a string", because the identity check is where a missing `schema` is first observed.
- FINDING: the reorder made `axerror.decodeClosedDocument`'s trailing-content guard unreachable - `parseEnvelopeIdentity` settles it on the same bytes first - so the guard moved rather than being left as a branch no test could redden. Same call the 0742 entry made about the leading-quote guard in `decodeExitStatus`.
- DECISION: §17.2 was rebound from `internal/config`'s `EncodeCurrent` to `Read` and stays **`unevidenced` at 0/1**. Its single scanner-visible clause is an unknown-*event* obligation; an unknown error code and an inert unknown extension are not an unknown Session Event, and no event-history retention exists here. The four numbered reader rules that binding does implement carry no RFC 2119 keyword and are invisible to the scanner. Moving the owner to the reader is a truthfulness fix, not a coverage gain - the ratio did not move.
- EVIDENCE: `acceptance_cases` 65 → 73 (74 after the 1157 rework), bindings 49, `clauses_discharged` 17/403 unchanged, digest re-pinned. Package coverage `cliresult` 95.5%, `axerror` 99.5%; the two remaining uncovered branches in `client.go` are unreachable by construction and `TestEveryRegisteredCommandMajorBindsAnErrorVersion` proves it over all 44 registered tags rather than leaving them untested.
- STATUS: Ready for review. TASK-260830-33sfxc, leaf 3 of 3 on `task-board/story/STORY-260830-2jylym`.

### 1015 — encoding/json is not a validator: it substitutes U+FFFD for a byte the contract requires to be valid UTF-8

- SCOPE: `internal/cliresult` (new package, TASK-260830-1bipsa), CLI Result §14.2 with the §17.2 reader rules and the §15.2 exit mapping.
- ROOT CAUSE: the first revision built the owned body by `json.Marshal` → `Canonicalize` → re-parse, on the assumption that anything the strict canonicalizer admits is inside the common data model. It is, but the *input* never reaches the canonicalizer intact: `encoding/json` silently replaces invalid UTF-8 bytes with U+FFFD rather than failing, so a caller string of `\xed\xa0\x80` became a conforming three-character replacement string and was admitted. §1.6 says "text MUST be valid UTF-8"; substituting a replacement character for a byte the caller supplied is a silent data change dressed as a validation pass.
- FIX: `walkInputValue` admits exactly the Go forms of the §1.6 model — nil, bool, string, `json.Number`, the integer types, `[]any`, `map[string]any` — and refuses everything else *by Go type* before marshalling. A custom `json.Marshaler` can emit any bytes it likes, so a check on the encoded form takes whatever the type chose to say; `TestInputWalkRefusesATypeOutsideTheCommonDataModel` pins that with a `spoofedString` that marshals to a perfectly valid `"payments-api"`.
- DECISION: the writer and the reader share **one** validator over **one** value model. `New` rebuilds the caller's body from its canonical bytes and both paths then run `validateBody`, so the writer structurally cannot emit an object its own `Decode` refuses — the defect class CR-TASK-260830-34elja-1 found in `axerror`, removed by construction rather than by a test. `TestEveryImplementedCommandRoundTripsThroughItsOwnReader` drives all eighteen tags through it.
- DECISION: where §14.2 states no rule, this package states none. `attach` and `pane` are in neither `operation_id` set the section names and neither is a pure read, so their nullability is `idUnconstrained`; `PathDiff` and `CLIFinding` carry no ID, so "sorted bytewise by that ID" does not reach them and `requireUnorderedArray` exists so that no ordering is imposed where none is declared. Inventing a sort refuses conforming documents exactly as surely as omitting one admits non-conforming ones — the BUG-260902-2faftr shape read from the other side.
- DECISION: the takeover adoption rule needs a session kind the takeover body does not carry. `New` takes it as a required constructor argument and refuses it for every other tag; `Decode` cannot have it and exposes `VerifyTakeoverAdoption` instead of skipping a MUST and returning an object indistinguishable from a checked one.
- FINDING: two mutants survive and are **declared subsumed** rather than tolerated. The SessionSummary `capabilities` count bound `[0..7]` is unreachable because the capability vocabulary has exactly seven members, so a map of admitted names can never exceed it; and `decodeRequiredUUIDv7OrNull`'s presence guard is unreachable because `decodeClosedDocument` already refuses a document missing any of the eight declared members. Both keep an assertion pinning the invariant that makes them subsumed, so the day the vocabulary grows the subsumption claim reddens.
- FINDING: the tag/version agreement check first looked killed and was not. `Decode(1.0.0, command="session.clone.run")` errors either way, because the unimplemented-body refusal message also contains "selects CLI Result 2.0.0". The decisive case is a tag whose body **is** built carried inside another registered version — a 1.0.0 takeover body inside a 2.0.0 envelope — where nothing else in the reader objects. Same shape as the `axerror` registry-as-its-own-oracle finding: an assertion that passes for a reason other than the one it claims.
- EVIDENCE: §14.2 8/9 clauses discharged, `partial`, still refused by assigned-scope admission and added to both refusal disclosure tables with its exact ratio. Clause `14.2#6` — the process exit status must equal the failure's `exit_code` — is **not** claimed: `Emit` and `ExitStatus` return that exact status and no `ax` binary exists to call `os.Exit` with it, so the process-level half is disclosed as unowned. Repository `clauses_discharged` 9/394 → 17/403, `acceptance_cases` 53 → 65, bindings 48 → 49. Package coverage 95.3%; 50 mutants, each verified applied and compiled before measurement, 48 of 48 non-subsumed killed.
- FIX (residual R2 from leaf 1's review): the Section 15.2 exit-code table has **eighteen** body rows - `[0, 2-17, 130]` - and this repository called it "nineteen" in four places, one of them already landed in `main`. Prose beside a table that measures itself drifts, so the count is no longer written: `TestExitStatusRegistryMatchesThePinnedTableRowForRow` reads the rows out of `internal/specdoc` through `TableRowAt`/`SectionID` and compares the count and every status against `exitMeanings` and against a reviewed literal set. `registry.go`, `README.md` (x2), `traceability.go` and `traceability_test.go` corrected; the §15.2 ownership gap now says eighteen and names the measuring test.
- FIX (residual R1 from leaf 1's review): `minRedactableCause = 8` decides which causes the causal-leak gate scans at all, and every cause in the corpus was at least 64 characters, so raising it to 16 or 64 left the suite green - the bound was proved by deleting the gate rather than by narrowing it. `TestMinimumRedactableCauseIsPinnedAtItsBoundary` now uses a cause of exactly 8 characters that must be scanned and one of 7 that must be skipped, on the message and on a diagnostic value, plus a multi-byte fixture that fails a byte-counted bound. Verified: 7, 9 and 16 each redden.
- NOTE: this supersedes the "nineteen-row" statements in the 0559 and earlier entries below. The implementation and its reviewed literal were always correct at 18; only the prose was wrong, and it had already been copied twice by the time it was caught.
- STATUS: Ready for review. TASK-260830-1bipsa, leaf 2 of 3 on `task-board/story/STORY-260830-2jylym`, one commit past the checkpoint.

### 0753 — Validate-then-alias is a bypass path, not a gate; and a clause claimed against the wrong actor

- SCOPE: `internal/axerror` rework after review of CR-TASK-260830-34elja-1 rev1 returned `changes_requested` with two blocking findings, both reproduced by the reviewer.
- ROOT CAUSE: **a validator that checks a graph the constructed value does not own has run and then let the checked data change.** `New` validated the caller's `Details` map and kept it through a *shallow* `cloneDetails`; `Detail` handed the live nested container straight back. Only the top-level map was isolated. Through the package's own exported accessor - no cooperation from the constructing call site needed - a validated failure could be made to emit `"password":"hunter2"`, 33,036 bytes against a declared 16 KiB bound, a value 7 containers deep against a declared bound of 4, and a Go `int` that `validateDetailValue` refuses outright. `axerror.Decode` then rejected the package's own `MarshalJSON` output.
- FINDING: every §15.1 detail bound was defeated by this, and **no test covered post-construction mutation** - all 20 mutants and the whole positive/negative suite only ever validated maps nobody touched afterwards. The exported doc comment claimed "the closed Section 15.1 object is the only shape this type can produce", which this board treats as a contract; it was false and undisclosed. `internal/config` already returns an isolated resolved-root snapshot for exactly this reason, so the convention existed and was not followed.
- FIX: deep copy on the way in (`cloneDetails` → `cloneDetailValue`) and on the way out (`Detail`). Five new mutants, three of them **narrowings** rather than deletions - copy shallow again, deep copy stopped at one level, deep copy applied to maps but not arrays - and the tests attack containers at three depths and on both sides of an array so a one-level copy fails them. `internal/axerror/ownership_test.go`.
- ROOT CAUSE: **a clause was claimed against a topically related section instead of against the actor its MUST binds.** Ownership clause `15.1#6` at SPEC.md:11047 was discharged by `LocalFromUntrusted`. That line's only RFC 2119 sentence is "The plugin MUST NOT masquerade a different-major object as v2" - it binds the **provider plugin**, a child process this repository does not build; its only binaries are `cataloggen` and `tracecheck`. The host half of the same table row, which `LocalFromUntrusted` does implement, carries no RFC 2119 keyword at all, so it is not what the clause scanner measured.
- FINDING: the scanner counts lines matching an RFC 2119 keyword; it cannot decide **which noun** the MUST governs. A human reading the subject of the sentence is the only check there is. This is the same ground on which `15.1#5` was correctly declared undischarged in the same registry - the honest refusal and the inflated claim shipped side by side.
- FIX: `15.1#6` dropped, the §15.1 `gap` now names both undischarged clauses with their reasons, digest pin recomputed. §15.1 6/7 → **5/7**, repository `clauses_discharged` 10/394 → **9/394**. This supersedes the EVIDENCE line of the 0742 entry below. No gate outcome moves: §15.1 stays `partial` and assigned-scope admission still refuses it.
- NOTE: the 0742 entry credits a leading-quote guard in `decodeExitStatus` as the fix for the `"9"` string-exit-status defect. It was not - the real fix was reading `exit_code` from `json.RawMessage`, and the guard was unkillable dead weight in front of `strconv.ParseInt`, which already refuses quoted bytes. Guard removed, the refusal table widened to `"9"`, `""`, `9.5`, `1e1`, `true`, `[]`, `{}`, `[9]`, and a mutant that strips quotes before parsing - the exact narrowing a `json.Number` field would reintroduce - now reddens.
- NOTE: rev1's harness verified several deletion mutants by grepping for an **unrelated** string that was present either way, which cannot distinguish "mutation applied" from "perl no-op". `run_mutant` now takes `present|absent` and a deletion is verified by the absence of the exact text it deleted.
- EVIDENCE: 26 mutants, each verified applied before measurement, 26 killed, 0 survivors, final restore re-verified uncached. `go test ./... -count=1` exit 0 across 12 packages; `go build`, `go vet`, `gofmt -l` clean; `tracecheck` exit 0 at 9/394.
- STATUS: Ready for review. TASK-260830-34elja rework, leaf 1 of 3 on `task-board/story/STORY-260830-2jylym`.

### 0742 — A key-name blacklist, again: the same shape BUG-260902-2faftr removed, caught by reading the logbook rather than by a test

- SCOPE: `internal/axerror` (new package, TASK-260830-34elja), Structured Error §15.1-15.3.
- ROOT CAUSE: the first revision of the detail-redaction gate matched key **substrings** — `token`, `socket`, `credential`, `secret`, `pid`, `named_pipe`, ... — against §16.2's whole exclusion table. Two errors in one rule. It refused `token_count`, `socket_timeout_ms` and `credential_profile`, ordinary diagnostics the pinned document admits, while admitting a secret written under any innocuous name: false-positive surface with no true-positive capability. And it applied §16.2, which governs manifests and bundles generated by `ax`, to §15.1 error **details**, whose declared prohibition is exactly four classes — "No detail may contain a credential, raw transcript, environment secret, or opaque bundle content". A detail key naming a PID, a tmux socket or a Terminal Instance Binding is none of those four; refusing it invented a constraint.
- FINDING: identical shape to BUG-260902-2faftr (`hasForbiddenConfigName`), one section over and one day later, in code written by someone who had not yet read the entry. It survived the whole positive/negative suite and 17 killed mutants, because every test asserted the gate was *reachable* and none asked which pinned line *declared* it. Nothing about mutation score detects an invented constraint: a mutant that deletes an unlicensed refusal correctly reddens the test that pins the unlicensed refusal.
- FIX: exact-match only, scoped to §15.1's four classes, 27 keys, each row carrying the class it names. `details.go:refuseExcludedKey`. The admitted rows in `TestDetailsRefuseExcludedClasses` are now the load-bearing half: `token_count`, `socket_timeout_ms`, `credential_profile`, `api_key_id`, `pid`, `tmux_socket_path`, `terminal_instance_binding`. A mutant restoring substring matching reddens on those and on nothing else.
- FINDING: **the registry was its own oracle.** The first version of that test ranged over the production `excludedDetailKeys` map, so the mutant that deletes `password` also deletes its own test case — SURVIVED, at exit 0, with 19 of 20 other mutants killed around it. Fixed by pinning the 27 rows as a reviewed literal table compared against production, the same shape `TestExitStatusRegistryIsClosed` already used for §15.2. General: a table-driven test that iterates the table it is testing measures nothing about the table's contents.
- FINDING: `exit_code` declared as `*json.Number` admits the JSON **string** `"9"` — `encoding/json` will unmarshal a quoted value into a `json.Number`. A peer could have written its exit status as text and been believed. Now decoded from `json.RawMessage` with a leading-quote refusal, so the JSON type is part of the check. Found by a negative row written to raise branch coverage, not by design review.
- DECISION: retryability is a refusal table and never a permission. `RetryabilityRefusal` reports only where the pinned document forbids `retryable = true` — exit classes 7, 16, 130 and the codes `operation_uncertain`, `terminal_backend_stale_generation`, `transaction_unknown` — each quoting the sentence that disqualifies it. It never reports that a retry is safe. The complement is not evidence.
- DECISION: `LocalFromUntrusted` refuses the Directory Node surface. §15.3 leaves its local code as `incompatible_protocol` or `adapter_protocol_violation`/`transport_failure` "as applicable" without fixing which, so the mapping is reported unknown rather than guessed into a literal that would then look normative.
- DECISION: §17.2 was **not** re-bound to this package despite being named in the task scope. Its single scanner-visible clause is an unknown-**event** reader obligation; an unknown error **code** is not an unknown session event. Its existing wrong binding to `internal/config/writer.go:EncodeCurrent` is left in place and left disclosed rather than rebound to a friendlier symbol.
- EVIDENCE: §15.1 6/7 and §15.3 2/3, both `partial` and both still refused by assigned-scope admission; §15.2 stays `unmeasured`. Repository `clauses_discharged` 2/394 → 10/394, `acceptance_cases` 43 → 53. Package coverage 99.7%. 20 mutants, each verified present before measurement, 20 killed, harness aborts if a restore leaves the baseline red.
- NOTE: the harness itself bit once. Run 1 aborted mid-script on a zsh `read-only variable: status` error, leaving mutant 1 applied; run 2 then backed up the mutated tree and every later result was measured against a registry missing exit class 7. The tally looked plausible — all KILLED — and was wrong. `mutants.sh` now refuses to start on a red baseline and re-verifies green after every restore.
- STATUS: Ready for review. TASK-260830-34elja, leaf 1 of 3 on `task-board/story/STORY-260830-2jylym`, one commit past the checkpoint.

### 0626 — Third instance of one class in one comment: the pin proved the digit and never the noun

- ROOT CAUSE: the 0559 fix rewrote `Canonicalize`'s intro to say it rejects "any document that **opens** more than 256 containers". `maxNestingDepth` bounds containers *open at once*. Driven through the real exported entry point, `[[],[],…×400]` opens 401 containers at depth 2 and canonicalizes to 1201 bytes with `err=<nil>`; `{"k0":{},…×400}` likewise. The accurate wording already existed 135 lines above, on the constant itself — "may be open at once" — and the rewrite restated it in the exported doc while dropping the three words that made it true.
- FINDING: N5's class, third time, same comment. rev1 shipped it as a false *behaviour* claim, rev1→rev2 fixed it into a false *citation*, rev2 shipped it as a false *refusal* claim about containers. Each fix was correct about the thing it was fixing and reintroduced the class one clause over. The 0559 entry rejects this pattern one paragraph before it recurred.
- FINDING: **the pin blessed it.** `TestCanonicalizeDocumentedContainerLimitIsTheEnforcedOne` string-matched `"more than 256 containers"` and then drove only *nested* arrays at 256 and 257 — evidence consistent with a depth bound and with a count bound alike. It proved the number and never the quantity the number bounds. A test that is green under both readings of the claim it pins is not pinning the claim; and this was the one positive-path-shaped assertion in a file built to avoid exactly that.
- FIX: the clause states "holds more than 256 containers open at once", and the assertion moved out of free prose into the row form this file already uses for rounding — `400 sibling arrays open 401 containers, 2 at once: accepted`, `256 nested … 256 at once: accepted`, `257 nested … 257 at once: refused`. Each row is built from its own text, both quantities are **re-measured from the document bytes** rather than recomputed from the shape that produced them, and the verdict is driven through `Canonicalize`. Three coverage requirements must stay satisfiable by the rows: the limit is reachable, the limit is enforced, and one accepted row opens more than the limit — the third is the fact that makes a count reading false, so shrinking the block back to the nested rows reddens instead of quietly restoring the ambiguity.
- FINDING: the qualifier is checked at *every* occurrence in the prose, not once. `strings.Contains` is satisfied by a comment that states the clause correctly here and incorrectly there — the same presence-check hole that let mutant N8 add a false SPEC clause beside the true one at 0559. Rows are exempt from the scan because they are not prose: a row that misstates the quantity fails on behaviour.
- EVIDENCE: ten mutants, each confirmed present in the source before measuring, all exit 1, no survivors. R1 reverts to the rev2 count wording; R2 flips the sibling row's verdict; R3 deletes the sibling row (delete-only, the coverage requirement fires); R4 lies about the row's own arithmetic, 401→402; R5 drifts the digit 256→512; **R6 narrows production into a genuine count bound** — refuse when `[`+`{` exceed `maxNestingDepth` — which keeps the doc's number true and reddens only because the noun is now driven; R7 adds a false count claim beside the true clause; R8 drifts a rounding row (parser regression control for the new row form); R9 narrows the depth guard to `maxNestingDepth-1`; R10 renames the test the comment cites.
- NOTE: general property. When a documented bound names a number and a quantity, a pin that varies only the number cannot distinguish the two readings. Vary the quantity: drive a case that is far past the number under one reading and nowhere near it under the other.
- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/canonical.go` (comments only), `internal/canonicaljson/canonical_number_contract_test.go`, `README.md`, `LOGBOOK.md`. No production logic changed; `canonical_test.go` and `internal/specdoc/` byte-for-byte unchanged. Package coverage 97.2%, unchanged.
- RESIDUAL: unchanged and still real — claims about RFC 8785 itself stay unmeasured, and free prose outside the enumerated rows, names and citations is still unchecked. The container clause is the second demonstration that this bound has a cost rather than being hypothetical; the treatment each time is to move the claim into a driven row, not to widen the disclaimer.
- ANOMALY: `curator status --check` still exits 1 with `worktree: go-testing-tools not-installed`, pre-existing Story-worktree provisioning state; this diff touches no Curator-managed path. Reported failing, not explained away.
- STATUS: Ready for review. BUG-260903-3nt3ah rev3.

### 0559 — The fix for a false claim shipped a false citation, and the bound that let it through said "unmeasurable" where the package already measured

- ROOT CAUSE: the 0553 fix rewrote `Canonicalize`'s doc comment and attributed "before identity calculation" to normative fixture `NUM-UNSAFE-ROUND`. That phrase is the `NUM-UNSAFE-NUMBER` row, `SPEC.md:301`; `NUM-UNSAFE-ROUND` is `SPEC.md:302` and says "Reject from the JSON number token before conversion to a host double". `LOGBOOK.md` repeated the false quotation. N5 was "an exported doc comment asserts a refusal the function does not perform"; its fix was "an exported doc comment asserts a citation the specification does not contain" — the same class, moved from behaviour to citation.
- FINDING: the decision survives and is strengthened. `NUM-UNSAFE-ROUND`'s real text describes this implementation more exactly than the row that was quoted, because `validateAXNumbers` refuses on the `json.Number` token before any conversion to a host double. Both fixtures are now cited, each from its own row.
- FINDING: the enabling defect was not the misquote, it was the disclosure under it. `canonical_number_contract_test.go` declared the specification quotations "which no test can verify against their sources from inside this package" while `constraint_excerpt_test.go` in the same package already imported `internal/specdoc` and did exactly that. An unmeasured area reported as unmeasurable — "nothing is there" where the truth was "nothing this checker looked at". The RFC 8785 half of that sentence is genuinely unverifiable in-repo and stays; the SPEC half became a gate.
- FIX: the doc comment now carries `SPEC.md:<line> [FIXTURE] "verbatim text"` rows parsed out of the comment itself and checked against the digest-pinned document. Four conditions per citation: the quote occurs in `SPEC.md`, **exactly once**, beginning at the declared line, and when a fixture is named it is the row `TableRowAt` says declares that fixture. Uniqueness is what stops a citation being *weakened* instead of corrected — `"Reject"` still begins at `SPEC.md:302` and now fails, because it also begins at nine other lines.
- FINDING: presence checks cannot see an added false claim. The first draft required the doc to mention the clause its citations land in, via `strings.Contains`. Mutant N8 — adding "in Section 1.5" beside the true "Section 1.6" — **survived green**. Rewritten as set equality between the clauses the comment names and the clauses the citations land in; N8 then reddens with `names SPEC.md clauses [1.5 1.6], but every citation lands in [1.6]`. A gate that only confirms the truth is present is not a gate against lies being added.
- EVIDENCE: nine mutants, each confirmed present in the source before measuring, all exit 1. N1 re-points a fixture attribution to the neighbouring row; N2 re-points the line; N3 weakens the quote to a non-unique fragment; N4 deletes the citation rather than correcting it (required-citation set fires); N5 cites a fixture row without naming the fixture; N6 drifts the documented container limit from `maxNestingDepth`; N7 renames the Appendix B pin the comment names; N8/N9 falsify the clause claim by addition and by substitution.
- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/canonical.go` (comments only), `internal/canonicaljson/canonical_number_contract_test.go`, `README.md`, `LOGBOOK.md`. Secondaries fixed: the stale `canonical.go:199` citation in the 0553 entry now names `prepareObjectIdentity` instead of a line number that had already moved to 256; the comment states `256` rather than the unexported `maxNestingDepth`, tied to the constant and to accept-at-256/refuse-at-257 through the real entry point.
- RESIDUAL: claims about RFC 8785 itself stay unmeasured. `TestCanonicalizeMatchesEveryFiniteRFC8785AppendixBNumberSample` catches drift between this implementation and a **vendored transcription** of Appendix B; drift between that transcription and the RFC is not detectable here, because RFC 8785 is not vendored. Stated in the file as a bound rather than as an absence.
- STATUS: Ready for review. BUG-260903-3nt3ah rev2.

### 0553 — A doc comment that promises a refusal is only pinnable if the doc is the test's input

- ROOT CAUSE: `Canonicalize`'s doc comment claimed it "rejects malformed UTF-8, invalid surrogate escapes, duplicate object names, and non-I-JSON input". Three of the four were true. The fourth was not: RFC 8785 Section 3.2.2.3 serializes numbers through the ECMAScript `Number.prototype.toString` algorithm on the binary64 value, so `canonical.go` rounded `9007199254740993` to `9007199254740992`, `18446744073709551615` to `18446744073709552000`, and `1.0` to `1`. No live defect — the identity path is gated by `validateAXNumbers`, called from `prepareObjectIdentity` — but `Canonicalize` is exported, so the first external caller would have inherited a guarantee it does not provide. Audit finding N5, the last of the thirteen second-audit findings.
- DECISION: option (b), document the rounding as intended, not (a), make it refuse. Both authorities the AC names point the same way. RFC 8785 Appendix B publishes the rounded outputs as *normative test data* and two of its samples are this exact rounding (`0x4340000000000000` → `9007199254740992`, `0x4430000000000000` → `295147905179352830000`), pinned at `canonical_test.go:47-70`; refusing in `Canonicalize` means refusing to be an RFC 8785 canonicalizer and deleting that test. The pinned SPEC never put the refusal there: it puts it on the AX *decoder*: fixture `NUM-UNSAFE-NUMBER` (SPEC.md:301) says "Reject **before identity calculation** with `incompatible_schema`", and `NUM-UNSAFE-ROUND` (SPEC.md:302) says "Reject **from the JSON number token before conversion to a host double**; an implementation that first rounds it to 9007199254740992 is nonconforming". Identity calculation is `CalculateObjectIdentity`/`VerifyObjectIdentity`, and `validateAXNumbers` runs inside `prepareObjectIdentity` before any canonical bytes exist. The implementation already conformed; only the prose lied.
- FINDING: a doc comment cannot be pinned by a test that *restates* it — that just moves the same unverified claim into a second file where it can drift identically. The pin only works if the comment is the test's **input**. `canonical_number_contract_test.go` parses the `literal -> canonical` rows and the exported entry-point names out of the `Canonicalize` doc with `go/parser` and checks them against real behaviour and against the entry-point set derived from the production call graph. Editing prose without code reddens (M3, M4); editing code without prose reddens (M5). An indented line in the comment that is neither a row nor an exported name is a hard failure, so documented-but-unchecked text cannot accumulate.
- FINDING: the negative test needed a carrier no other validator would catch. `extensions` is it — `validateExtensionValue` in `closed_shapes.go:1833` accepts any `json.Number` unexamined — so an unsafe integer smuggled through an otherwise valid Session Record can only be refused by `validateAXNumbers`. A green baseline with a safe extension number runs first, or the three refusals would prove nothing.
- REGRESSION AVOIDED: the first draft derived its own AST call graph recording only `call.Fun.(*ast.Ident)` edges and skipping methods — the identical construction the 0210 entry measured at 3 of 7 UTF-8 guards while it looked complete. Rewritten to reuse `productionCallGraph`/`exportedProductionEntryPoints`/`reachableProductionFunctions` from `utf8_subsumption_test.go`. Shipping a second, weaker graph beside the hardened one would have reopened a blind spot this board had already paid to close, in the same package, three hours later.
- EVIDENCE: six mutants, each confirmed present in the source before its test was measured. M1 **narrows** rather than deletes — `integer > maxSafeInteger` → `maxSafeInteger+2`, gate still in place, bound moved by two — and the negative test still catches `9007199254740993`. M2 deletes the gate. M3/M4 drift the doc. M5 makes `Canonicalize` call `validateAXNumbers` and the derived set correctly grows to include `CanonicalByteLength`. M6 registers `validateAXNumbers` in a function-value table and routes the gate through it: **behaviour unchanged, `go build` exit 0, negative test still green at exit 0**, and only `TestValidateAXNumbersHasNoCallSiteThisGraphCannotSee` reddens.
- FINDING: M6 is what separates a stated bound from a held one. The graph resolves a computed callee to every function whose identifier is used as a value in the package, so the derived entry-point set is exact *only while* `validateAXNumbers` is never used as a value. That precondition is not narration — it is the single test standing between a dispatch-table registration and a silently widened set with the whole suite `ok`.
- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/canonical.go` (comments only — `Canonicalize`, `CalculateObjectIdentity`, `VerifyObjectIdentity`, `validateAXNumbers`; no logic changed), `internal/canonicaljson/canonical_number_contract_test.go` (new), `README.md`. `canonical_test.go` byte-for-byte unchanged. Package coverage 97.2%, unchanged.
- ANOMALY: `curator status --check` exits 1 with `worktree: go-testing-tools not-installed`. Pre-existing Story-worktree provisioning state — `.claude/skills/` does not exist in the worktree at all and this diff touches no Curator-managed path. Reported failing, not explained away.
- STATUS: Ready for review. BUG-260903-3nt3ah. Closes the second adversarial audit's thirteenth and last finding.

### 0412 — Symbol reachability is not semantic coverage, and 48 bindings measured 2/394

- ROOT CAUSE: `internal/traceability` proved two things about a `section_binding` — the named Go declaration parses and exists, and the linked acceptance case is registered with an executable test. Neither compares the implementation against the section it claims, so a sliver satisfied a whole-section binding and `tracecheck -section X` reported it as owned. `section:2.2` (22 unconditional lease/replica invariants) was bound to `validateSessionRecordCommon`, which validates one enum and a name grammar; `section:18.4` (audit retention) was bound to `OpenProjection`, and no retention code exists. Three independently produced branches reached that state without anyone noticing.
- FINDING: the consequence is not a wrong report, it is a licence to do nothing. Any future Story assigned §2.2 or §18.4 would have found the section already owned and could legitimately have closed with no work.
- FIX: every `section_binding` declares a `coverage` level and the gate recomputes it. Denominator measured from `internal/specdoc` — RFC 2119 clause lines under the heading *and its subheadings*, so a parent is measured over its children. Numerator = the clauses the binding enumerates, each of which must index that inventory, declare its exact `SPEC.md` line, quote it verbatim beginning on that line, and name an acceptance case the binding itself owns. Declared level must equal the measured bucket. Assigned-scope admission now requires `full`; repository-wide verification stays green on an honest registry.
- EVIDENCE: 48 bindings discharge **2 of 394** normative clauses. full=1, sliver=1, unevidenced=43, unmeasured=3, unowned=2. Assigned-scope admission succeeds only for §6.2. (Revision 1 also admitted §7.3, §13.14.5 and §15.2 on the false ground that their pinned sections carry no obligation of their own; see the entry below.)
- FINDING: `MAY`/`SHOULD` are excluded from the denominator deliberately. Counting them would inflate the ratio and make coverage look worse than it is, which is the same dishonesty in the other direction.
- ANOMALY: no shipped binding could honestly be declared `full` except §6.2, whose single clause is "terminal backend MUST be `conpty`" on native Windows — already discharged by `TestEveryPinnedReaderHasPositiveNativeWindowsAndWSL2Lanes` through the production `Load`. Without it the positive arm of the gate could only have been proven synthetically. A gate whose admit path is never exercised on shipped data is not much better than one that is always green.
- FINDING: the disclosure surfaced two more of the same shape that no audit had named. `section:17.2`'s single clause is an unknown-event *reader* obligation while its binding names the Configuration *writer* `EncodeCurrent`; `section:2.1`'s single clause is a replica runtime obligation nothing here implements. §6.5 kept its binding but its gap names a real defect: SPEC.md:2585 requires `required_capabilities` to default to the platform lane minimum, and `internal/config/validation.go:670` accepts only an empty default.
- DECISION: `unowned_sections` is a disclosure, never an exemption. An entry may not cover a section the generated catalog requires an owner for, so the existing "registered section_binding has no implementation owner" assertion keeps its full force; and every new field sits inside the reviewed projection digest, so a coverage claim cannot be self-minted.
- SCOPE STATED, NOT IMPLIED: the gate decides clause reality, quote fidelity, line attribution, case ownership, and label-vs-ratio. It does **not** decide that the named acceptance case exercises the clause's meaning — a complete enumeration pointed at one weak test is admitted. Named as the residual class in `README.md` and the artifact rather than left for a reader to discover. It also cannot see an obligation stated without an uppercase RFC 2119 keyword; revision 1 stated that limit as "those sections carry no obligation of their own", which was a different and false claim — corrected in the entry below.
- SCOPE: `internal/traceability/{traceability.go,ownership.v0.5.0.json,cmd/tracecheck}`, `internal/specdoc/specdoc.go` (its "only test binaries import it" claim became false), `README.md`. Three former positive-admission tests inverted into disclosure tables that pin each refusal by exact ratio.
- STATUS: Superseded by the entry below. Revision 1 closed the label bypass and opened a scanner bypass in the same change. BUG-260902-2m7slg.

### 0900 — "No RFC 2119 keyword" is a failure to measure, not an absence of obligation

- ROOT CAUSE: the coverage gate's obligation scanner matches uppercase `MUST`/`SHALL`/`REQUIRED` only. A section stating its obligations as a table scores zero clauses, was bucketed `declarative`, and `declarative` was **admitted** as an assigned scope with no clause evidence at all. Revision 1 of this gate therefore reproduced the exact bug it was built to remove, reached through the scanner instead of through the label.
- EVIDENCE: `go run ./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck -section 15.2` exited **0**. §15.2 is the nineteen-row normative exit-code registry (SPEC.md:11073-11095) and nothing in the tree implements it — the only `os.Exit` calls are the `exit(1)` paths of `cataloggen` and `tracecheck`. Same for `-section 7.3`, the closed Provider Manifest, whose sole trace is a catalog row naming its URN. Both were bound to `internal/catalog/catalog.go:ForRelease`, an unrelated symbol.
- MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED: the blind spot is **19 of the 157** pinned headings (7.3, 10.8.1, 13.5, 13.12, 13.14.1-13.14.5, 14.3, 14.6, 15.2, 16.6, 16.7, 18.2, 19.4, Appendices A/B/C). `TestUnmeasuredCoverageIsAScannerBlindSpotNotAnAbsenceOfObligation` re-derives that set from the pinned document and shows every one of them has a substantive body (8-272 non-blank lines). Not one is a heading with nothing to discharge, so the "declarative" reading was false for all 19, not just the two that were bound.
- AGGRAVATOR: `verifySectionBindingCoverage` *refused* a gap on the `declarative` bucket, so §15.2 was structurally forbidden from disclosing that nothing implements exit codes. The disclosure mechanism the change built was unavailable to exactly the bindings that needed it most.
- DECISION: the bucket is renamed `unmeasured`, it now owes the same mandatory gap as every level below `full`, and it is **dropped from the admitted set entirely** — admission requires `full` and nothing else. The alternative was to admit on a reviewed justification sentence, and that was rejected: the gate cannot verify such a sentence, so unlocking admission with one is self-minted evidence for precisely the class this gate exists to refuse. Absence of a scanner-visible clause is a failure to read, and a fallback defined for absence must not fire on a failure to read.
- NOT RE-BOUND: §7.3, §13.14.5 and §15.2 keep their bindings and carry honest gaps. `unowned_sections` was not an option — `expectedCatalogSectionBindings` requires an owner for all three — and re-binding them to a friendlier symbol is the failure this board has hit three times. §13.14.5's `validateSessionEventV2` is at least topically the section's subject and its gap says so; §7.3's and §15.2's say the implementation does not exist.
- FINDING: gap quality was `len >= 32 && strings.Contains(gap, display)`. "Section 9.2 is not fully covered here yet." satisfied it, and `Contains(gap, "6.5")` also matched "6.55". A gap must now name its section as a whole identifier and name the production declaration its binding is registered to. One shipped gap (§1.6, bound to `ErrInvalidScalar`) failed the new rule and was corrected. The residual is stated rather than hidden: a sentence naming both and still saying nothing is admitted.
- GUARD ADDED: `internal/specdoc` reaching non-test code made "the embedded document never reaches the ax command" a claim with nothing behind it. `TestEmbeddedDocumentNeverReachesAProductBinary` now reads the module import graph from source with `go/parser`, refuses any `main` outside `tracecheck` that can reach the package, and proves the detector by planting a `cmd/ax` that imports `internal/traceability`. `TestModuleHasNoProductCommandYet` fails when `ax` lands, forcing that decision to be deliberate.
- STATUS: Resolved. `go build`/`go vet`/`go test ./...` exit 0; measured ratio unchanged at 2/394 with the three bindings relabelled. BUG-260902-2m7slg rework, handed to review.

### 0210 — A derived call graph that only sees `f(x)` covers 3 of 7 guards and looks complete

- ROOT CAUSE: `utf8_subsumption_test.go` pinned "no exported entry point hands an already-decoded value to a `utf8.ValidString` re-check" over an AST call graph that recorded an edge only for `call.Fun.(*ast.Ident)` naming a top-level declaration, and skipped `Recv != nil` in both derivations. This package dispatches through a function-value table — validators registered in `mustBuildImmutableObjectShapeValidators` and invoked as `validator(object)` in `validateImmutableObjectShape` — so neither the registration nor the invocation was an edge.
- EVIDENCE: review mutant H, an exported `ValidateDecodedRecord(map[string]any)` reaching the guards through that table, left all three pins green and the whole package suite `ok`. Mutant I, the same body as an exported method, survived for the second reason. The producer's own passing mutant D2 called a guard by direct identifier — the single shape the graph could see. Measured coverage was 3/7 guarded functions; `validateSessionLaunchPlan`, `validateSessionBoardIdentity`, `validateSortedUniqueStrings` and `validateProviderIdentityRecord` were pinned vacuously.
- FINDING: an AST call graph is a claim about a *modelled subset* of dispatch, and its blind spot is invisible from inside — every test still passes, and the structure looks more thorough the longer it gets. A reachability pin must publish its coverage as a ratio; prose about what it checks cannot distinguish 7/7 from 3/7.
- DECISION: the graph now records edges for calls through a function value — a callee that is not a package function, a predeclared identifier, or a named type is treated as reaching every function whose identifier is used as a value in the package — and includes methods in both derivations. Coverage is now 7/7 and is asserted, not narrated: `TestEveryUTF8RecheckIsCoveredByTheEntryPointPin` fails and names the uncovered functions if any guard drops out of reach.
- DECISION: the published claim was narrowed in the same change. `closed_shapes.go` no longer says the invariant "cannot be broken silently" and `README.md` no longer says a map-taking entry point simply "reddens the pin". Both now state the modelled constructions and name what still evades them: reflection, a function value handed to another package, and a func-typed struct field, since `x.M(...)` resolves only when `M` is a method declared in this package. Fixing the graph without narrowing the claim moves a false guarantee one construction outward rather than removing it.
- EVIDENCE: mutants H, I and I2 (byte-parameter method — the receiver is still caller-supplied decoded state) all redden `TestNoExportedEntryPointHandsADecodedValueToAUTF8Recheck`, each naming all 7 guarded functions. Narrowing mutant K removes only the function-value edge: the clean tree stays green and mutant H survives, which is what identifies that edge as the decisive addition rather than something else in the rewrite. Mutant K2 removes the computed-callee edge as well and reproduces the review's measurement exactly — `coverage is 3/7`, naming the same four functions.
- FINDING: a guard that no mutant can redden is not covered, however thorough the derivation around it looks. The ratio is the honest report; "machine-checked" is not.
- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/utf8_subsumption_test.go`, `internal/canonicaljson/closed_shapes.go` (comment only), `README.md`. No production logic and no existing assertion changed; `internal/canonicaljson` coverage stayed at 97.2%.

### 0135 — The fidelity fix reproduced its own bug, one schema over

- ROOT CAUSE: the clause anchor holds a citation to the right numbered section; it cannot hold it to the right row of a table that declares several sibling schemas one per line. `SPEC.md:4787-4793` declares seven Git types one table row each, all in Section 10.4. Seven shipped enumeration rows cited a sibling's row: `GitIndex.format` quoted GitObjectPack's `format:git_pack_v2` while `validateGitIndex` (`closed_shapes.go:1454`) enforces `git_index`; `GitIndexEntry.mode` quoted GitHead's `mode:branch|detached|unborn` while `closed_shapes.go:1514` enforces `requireUint(object, "mode", 1<<32-1)`; `GitIndexEntry.oid` quoted GitHead's nullable `oid` while `requireGitOID` admits no null. Every rule then in place admitted them — verbatim text, correct line, correct clause, member name present as a substring — and `go test ./... -count=1` exited 0 with all seven shipped.
- FINDING: three of the seven were regressions introduced by 0022. The pre-change cells said `exact git_index`, `uint32`, and `git-oid; matches object format` — accurate prose. The rewrite replaced them with verbatim quotes of a different schema. Same failure class the Bug exists to remove, produced by the fix.
- FIX: a declaring-row anchor. `specdoc.TableRowAt` reports what a Markdown table body row declares — its table's first-column header and its own first cell. A citation landing on such a row must land on the row that declares what it cites: the first cell names the member (per-member `Field` tables), or the identifier under which the document declares that shape (`constraintRowDeclaringIdentifiers`, 15 shapes, asserted exactly against the artifact). Citations landing on prose are unconstrained by it and remain the residual gap.
- DECISION: two citations are exempted by name, line, and written reason rather than by widening the rule — both Session Record majors cite the Section 2.1 Terms row `Session name`, because the Session Record clause defers the name grammar to a section reference that contains no grammar. An unused exemption reddens, and removing either one reddens the shipped artifact, so they are load-bearing and disclosed, not decoration.
- FINDING: the blank-line block separator from 0022 did not stop a quote crossing the newline between two adjacent table rows, which is not a blank line. `Lease Record.holder_host_id` quoting its own row stitched to the `predecessor_lease_id` row after it was admitted by the whole package. A table row is a complete line by construction, so no honest excerpt spans two; the newline between two table lines is now a hard boundary too. The `README.md` and artifact claim that whitespace collapsing forgave hard wrapping and table indentation "and nothing else" was false in both files and is now stated as what it is.
- EVIDENCE: 14 sibling retargets derived from the document itself — every shared-member pair of the Section 10.4 Git table, both directions — each planted into a copy of the shipped artifact and each required to be refused by the sibling's name. Six mutants run in a throwaway `git archive` copy, all confirmed present before measurement: deleting the anchor reddens all 15 sibling and field plants; narrowing it to `Type` tables only reddens the field-table plant; restoring one of the seven pre-fix citations reddens and names the row and GitHead; removing the table-row boundary reddens both the specdoc and the artifact stitch tests; removing one declaring pin reddens 8 shipped rows rather than granting a free pass; a stale pin and a stale exemption both redden. Class re-scan over all 347 rows: 357 citations, 235 on table body rows, 0 naming another type, 2 naming another member and both exempted by name.
- SCOPE: `internal/specdoc/specdoc.go`, `specdoc_test.go`, `internal/canonicaljson/constraint_excerpt_test.go`, `testdata/constraint-enumeration.md` (7 cells + methodology), `README.md`. No production validator changed.
- STATUS: `internal/canonicaljson` 97.2%, `internal/specdoc` 100.0%. gofmt, `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`, `go test ./... -count=1`, `go test ./... -cover`, `tracecheck`, `go generate ./internal/catalog` all exit 0. Ready for review.

### 0050 — Correcting one gate's input silently narrowed another gate

- ROOT CAUSE: `sessionRecordGrammarFamily` classified a constraint enumeration row into an executable grammar family by substring-matching the row's `Pinned SPEC declaration` prose. That column is the fidelity artifact 0022 rewrote. Replacing informative-but-partly-fabricated prose with verbatim `SPEC.md` fragments removed the words the classifier greps for, so eight rows fell out of `TestSessionRecordDeclaredGrammarRowsReachIdentityProductionEntries` and stopped being attacked with invalid grammar values through both public identity entries. Measured on both trees with the same command: 18 rows -> 11, 107 subtests -> 66, `reverse-dns` 11 -> 4.
- FINDING: the completeness check was `familyCounts[family] == 0`. Four is not zero, so a 64% narrowing of the largest family reported nothing. A non-empty guard on a derived set is the same defect class as the non-empty guard on the excerpt column that 0022 exists to remove — one level up, in the gate doing the removing.
- FINDING: `Session Record Launch Plan.env_names` was a pure regression. Its new excerpt at `:1490` quotes the grammar verbatim, `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]{0,127}` included; nothing about its fidelity changed. It left only because the classifier greps for the words "environment names", which the true quote does not contain.
- FINDING: the seven `extensions` rows that left — Board Goal, Board Identity, Fork Provenance, and the four derivation-provenance variants — were being attacked on the strength of a declaration the pinned document does not make for them. `SPEC.md` states the reverse-DNS key rule as a local table row only for Launch Plan `:1493`, Task-board Reference `:1512`, and the two Session Record majors `:1481`; elsewhere it names `extensions` in a prose member list and never restates the rule. The pre-fix cell "object; reverse-DNS extension keys only" was itself the cross-schema inference this work exists to remove.
- DECISION: keep all eleven, keyed on production rather than on a quote. Production routes every `extensions` object through one shared `validateExtensionsObject` (`closed_shapes.go:1784`), and `README.md` claims that for every open extensions map, so per-shape reachability is a claim worth attacking whatever the document restates locally. The row records what the document does and does not say; the gate records what production enforces. Conflating those two is what produced the defect in the first place.
- FIX: `sessionRecordGrammarRows` pins the row set by shape and member — the two fields a fidelity correction cannot rewrite — and `sessionRecordGrammarFamilyCounts` pins a per-family count. An absent pinned row is reported by name instead of skipped. `provider-id` is now shape-aware: the `Session Record 2.0.0 and 3.0.0` row was driving a v1 record.
- EVIDENCE: `TestSessionRecordGrammarRowSetRefusesASilentlyNarrowedEnumeration` removes one row at a time from the shipped enumeration — `env_names`, and two of the seven `extensions` rows — and requires the report to name the dropped row and the family count to fall. These are narrowings, not deletions: the gate stays present, reachable, and green on all remaining rows. `TestSessionRecordGrammarClassificationIgnoresPinnedSpecProse` replaces every row's declaration cell with `L1 "AX Specification"` and requires the classification, the row count, and every family count to be unchanged — the F1 mutant, executed.
- FINDING: `specdoc.Normalize` collapsed blank lines along with every other whitespace run, so a "verbatim" quote could stitch the tail of one block to the head of the next. Admitted before the fix: `L4613 "<code>size</code> bytes: The descriptor is closed and contains exactly <code>schema</code>"` spans the blank line at `:4614`. Both halves are individually verbatim, so no other rule would have caught it.
- FIX: a whitespace run containing a blank line collapses to a block separator that `Normalize` can never emit, making the refusal structural rather than a caller special case. Zero of the 347 shipped rows relied on the old behaviour, so the tightening cost nothing. Pinned by `TestQuoteLinesRefusesTextThatSpansABlankLine` and a planted stitched row in `TestPlantedConstraintEnumerationRowsRedden`, now 13 cases.
- FINDING: the excerpt gate was shape-blind. Retargeting `ManifestEntry.file.size` from `:4746` to BlobChunk's bounded `:4622` clause — a different schema, a bound that member does not carry — left the whole package green. Same cross-schema class as the `environment_id` finding in 0022, which was found by reading the document, not by the gate.
- FIX: anchor the citation to a clause. `specdoc.SectionID` resolves a line to its nearest enclosing numbered heading (an unnumbered subheading opens no clause), and each shape pins the clauses its citations may use. Measured before pinning: all 40 shapes' citations already clustered in exactly one clause, except the two Session Record majors, which legitimately cite 2.1 as well as 5.1 for the Terms-table name grammar. The pinned shape set is asserted exactly against the artifact, so a new shape must declare its clause. `TestClauseAnchorRefusesEveryForeignSectionForOneRow` plants a verbatim line from each of the 11 other clauses into one row: 11 of 11 refused, shipped row still admitted.
- NOTE: the residual limit is stated in the artifact and `README.md` rather than left implied. The anchor is a clause, not a shape — ten shapes are declared in 10.4 — so a retarget *within* a clause is still admitted, and `strings.Contains(text, row.member)` still matches a member name embedded in another schema's identifier. The `environment_id` case sits inside that gap: both of its lines are in 7.8.
- NOTE: general property. When a gate's input is an artifact another gate rewrites, membership in the first gate must be keyed on a field the rewrite cannot touch, and its completeness assertion must be a pinned count rather than a non-empty check. Otherwise correcting one axis quietly disarms the other, and both stay green.
- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/session_record_versions_test.go`, `constraint_excerpt_test.go`, `testdata/constraint-enumeration.md` (methodology section only — no row's shape, member, constraint, call site or excerpt changed), `internal/specdoc/specdoc.go` (block separator, `SectionID`), `specdoc_test.go`, `README.md`. No production validator changed.
- STATUS: Resolved. BUG-260902-3dn8jd revision 2, addressing review F1 (blocking) and recording F2 and F3.

### 0035 — Stale line numbers turned a four-site audit into an eight-site class

- ROOT CAUSE (part 1): `SPEC.md:303-304` publishes SHA-256 digests for `NUM-U64-STRING` (`{"n":"9007199254740992"}`) and `NUM-U64-MAX` (`{"n":"18446744073709551615"}`) and nothing in this repository recomputed either. Only `NUM-SAFE-MAX` was pinned. The `decimal_uint64` SYNTAX was well tested; the published DIGEST claim — the thing a content-addressed system actually promises — was not.
- DECISION: expected values quoted from `SPEC.md` extracted at the pinned commit `28bf96d7dd7ebf3cd9e2ccd91d35b8660699dd5c`, whose SHA-256 was verified equal to `internal/specpin.DocumentSHA256` before the lines were read. Both rows are quoted verbatim in the test comment. A value recomputed from this encoder would agree with any drift the encoder acquires.
- EVIDENCE (part 1): mutant A appends one byte to `Canonicalize`'s return and reddens both new assertions plus the two that already existed; mutants B2 and C2 change one byte of one fixture each and redden exactly that fixture's assertion. Individually decisive, so neither new assertion is carried by the other.
- ANOMALY: the audit located the part-2 sites as `closed_shapes.go:258, :1506, :1525, :1760`. None of those lines re-checks UTF-8 on any current ref. They match commit `7b94c9ad`, five commits back; the file has moved ~530 lines since. The same commit shows EIGHT `utf8.ValidString` sites, not four — the audit named a subset with no property distinguishing it. Resolved all eight rather than four, because "the clause sweep reports no unexplained survivor" is false while four identical survivors remain.
- FINDING: line numbers in a board finding decay faster than the finding does. Map them to a commit before trusting them, and re-derive the population rather than accepting the cited count.
- DECISION (part 2): kept and documented, not deleted. The subsumption is real — `decodeStrict` refuses non-UTF-8 input before any value exists — but it is a package-internal invariant, not a property of the eight validators. `CanonicalByteLength(value any)` already shows this package can export a non-`[]byte` entry point, so a future map-taking entry would silently make all eight comments false. Deleting would also rewrite refusal statements pinned in `refusal_guards_test.go`.
- FINDING: a first attempt pinned the invariant as "every exported entry point that reaches a re-check also reaches `decodeStrict`". Mutant G survived it: `validateExtensionsObject` reaches `canonicalByteBound` -> `CanonicalByteLength` -> `Canonicalize` -> `decodeStrict`, so transitive reachability of `decodeStrict` is satisfied by a call that never touched the caller's bytes. Reaching a decoder downstream is not the same fact as being decoded by it. Replaced with two properties that are each decidable: no exported entry point reaching a re-check may take a non-`[]byte` parameter, and `json.NewDecoder`/`json.Unmarshal` may appear only inside `decodeStrict`.
- EVIDENCE (part 2): 4 mutants, all killed. D2 adds an exported `map[string]any` entry point reaching the re-checks; G2 adds an exported `[]byte` entry point that decodes with `json.Unmarshal`; E3 deletes one subsumption comment; F3 is the narrowing mutant — the comment stays but names `requireExactMembers` instead of `decodeStrict`.
- DISCLOSED, NOT FIXED: `internal/canonicaljson/core_records.go:278` (`validateProviderIdentityRecord`) carries a ninth re-check of the same class, outside this bug's stated `closed_shapes.go` scope. Also, nothing derives the set of digest-publishing Section 1.6 fixtures from the pinned document — `SPEC.md` is not vendored — so a future added fixture cannot be caught as unpinned by a test.
- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/canonical_test.go`, `internal/canonicaljson/closed_shapes.go` (comments only), new `internal/canonicaljson/utf8_subsumption_test.go`, `README.md`.
- STATUS: BUG-260902-1874eo and sibling BUG-260902-9uwnm7 handed to review in one change. `gofmt`, `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`, `go test ./... -count=1`, `go test ./... -cover -count=1`, tracecheck, `go generate ./internal/catalog` + clean diff, and `FuzzCanonicalizeRoundTrip` at 100x all exit 0. `internal/canonicaljson` coverage 97.2%.

### 0022 — The enumeration proved it agreed with itself, and nothing else

- ROOT CAUSE: `internal/canonicaljson/constraint_inventory_test.go` derived shape, member and call site from the production `requireExactMembers` argument lists and compared them both ways, but required the `Pinned SPEC declaration` column only to be `!= ""`. Artifact and code could be wrong about the contract together and stay green. A self-consistency proof wearing fidelity-proof clothes.
- FINDING: measured all 347 pre-fix rows against the real pinned `SPEC.md` under whitespace-only normalization. 149 quoted text the document does not contain at all. 161 more quoted text that occurs in several places, so the cell identified no particular declaration. Only 37 resolved to exactly one line — and the old contract had no way to say even those were right. Not a few bad rows; the column was unfalsifiable by construction.
- FIX: `internal/specdoc` embeds the byte-exact `SPEC.md` of the pinned release, accepted only when its SHA-256 equals `specpin.DocumentSHA256`. Every row's cell is now `L<line> "<verbatim text>"` entries; the text must occur in the pinned document AND begin on the declared line, and at least one entry must name the member. `TestConstraintEnumerationSpecExcerptsQuoteThePinnedSpecification` plus `TestArtifactQuotesAreVerbatimPinnedSpecificationText`, which extends the same rule to every curly-quoted span elsewhere in the file.
- DECISION: vendor the document rather than fetch it. A test that reaches the network is not deterministic, and a live fetch of the spec default branch would compare against whatever that branch says today. Only test binaries import `specdoc`, so the 883 KiB embed never reaches a shipped command.
- DECISION: line anchoring, not just presence. In an 883 KiB document, "this text appears somewhere" is nearly free — `<code>schema</code>` alone occurs in ~60 places. The line is what turns a quote into a citation. The member-name anchor is the second half: without it a row could satisfy the gate with a true sentence about something else.
- DECISION: normalization is whitespace-only and stated in the artifact. Case, punctuation, digits and inline `<code>` markup compare exactly. `TestNormalizeForgivesOnlyWhitespace` pins it from the other side by requiring case-folded and backtick-for-`<code>` variants of real specification text to stay unmatched — a looser rule is how an excerpt drifts into unrelated text.
- FINDING: `validateEnvironmentTuple` enforces `[a-z][a-z0-9.-]{0,63}` on `environment_id` (`closed_shapes.go:733`), but the pinned EnvironmentTuple clause at `SPEC.md:3626-3630` gives that member no type and no format — exactly like the `environment_version`, `store_schema_fingerprint` and `adapter_version` siblings this package already refuses to infer constraints for. The only pinned statement of the grammar is `SPEC.md:3609`, a **Session Adapter Manifest** row. Same cross-schema inference as the 2322 entry, surviving one member over. Production left unchanged and the row now quotes both lines with the open question named; whether `environment-id` is a common type inferable across schemas is a spec question, not a test question.
- FINDING: the Session Record `name` row cited "Section 2.1 grammar", which appears nowhere in the document. `SPEC.md:1471` says "Section 2.3 grammar"; §2.3 contains no grammar; the grammar is written in the §2.1 Terms table at `:363`. The document's own indirection had been flattened into a claim it never makes. Both lines are now quoted, and `sessionRecordGrammarFamily` classifies that row by the quoted grammar rather than by the section reference — a stricter key.
- FINDING: `testdata/closed-vocabularies.md` has the same class of column and is still unchecked; its declarations paraphrase rather than quote. Its `validateSessionForkProvenance` `provider_fork_mode` row cites `:1653`, the same-provider-fork variant, not the Fork Provenance prose at `:1536-1537` its validator implements. Out of this Bug's scope; `specdoc` makes it cheap now.
- EVIDENCE: 11 planted defects, each mutating a copy of the shipped artifact and each required to redden — invented quote, invented quote among true ones, true quote at the wrong line, true quote that never names the member, paraphrase of a line that never names the member, paraphrase outside the document, empty quote, no line reference, the pre-fix bare-prose cell, empty cell, trailing text. Paired with `TestUnmodifiedConstraintEnumerationIsAdmitted` so the plants prove a bound rather than a broken parser. Two of them narrow rather than delete: wrong-line keeps real text and moves only the line; unnamed-member keeps a real line and removes only the anchor. `specdoc.Parse` separately refuses 7 document perturbations including a single-character and a whitespace-only substitution.
- SCOPE: `internal/specdoc/` (new package + vendored `SPEC.md`), `internal/canonicaljson/constraint_excerpt_test.go` (new), `constraint_inventory_test.go`, `session_record_versions_test.go`, `testdata/constraint-enumeration.md` (all 347 rows), `README.md`, `.spec/README.md`. No production validator changed.
- STATUS: `internal/canonicaljson` 97.2% unchanged, `internal/specdoc` 100.0%. gofmt, `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`, `go test ./... -count=1`, `-cover -count=1`, `tracecheck`, `go generate ./internal/catalog` + `git diff --exit-code` all exit 0. Ready for review.

## 2026-09-02

### 2322 — One regex, two schemas, two opposite right answers

- ROOT CAUSE: `semverPattern` in `internal/canonicaljson/closed_shapes.go` was applied to `EnvironmentTuple.adapter_version`, a member the pinned SPEC v0.5.0 declares with NO type and NO format. The SemVer word reaches it only through the Session Adapter Manifest row at `SPEC.md:3610`, which closes a different object, and the Probe sentence at `:3625` that equates "adapter version" to the verified Manifest — which names the Probe's own top-level member, not the nested tuple member. Cross-schema inference by field-name similarity, which §1.6 forbids. `1.2.3-rc.1` was refused with nothing in the contract rejecting it.
- FINDING: the same compiled regex was ALSO used at `validateMigrationExtensionObject` and `validateTerminalV4Payload`, where §17.3 and §1871-1872 DO declare `semver`. So the fix is not one answer applied twice; it is one rule producing opposite answers. The constraint applies exactly where the document declares it — nowhere at the tuple, everywhere it says `semver`.
- FINDING: at the sites where it IS declared, the pattern was wrong in the other direction. `^(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\.(...)\.(...)$` is not Semantic Versioning 2.0.0; it omits prerelease and build metadata entirely. The document names Semantic Version and spells out no grammar, so the only defensible reading is the whole standard. `internal/config/validation.go:21` already carried the full SemVer 2.0.0 spelling — the repo was internally inconsistent about its own type name.
- DECISION: constraint REMOVED at the tuple (presence-only, matching its untyped `environment_version` and `store_schema_fingerprint` siblings in the same clause); constraint KEPT and WIDENED to SemVer 2.0.0 in full everywhere the document types a member `semver`. Both halves written into `internal/canonicaljson/testdata/constraint-enumeration.md` as an explicit decision section with the §17.3 authorising line quoted verbatim, not left implied by the code.
- FINDING: widening the pattern cost 22 grammar-inventory dimensions (2 anchors, 18 character classes, 2 one-or-more quantifiers), each needing a witness that production refuses and that exactly its own one-dimension widening admits. Derived mechanically with a throwaway reimplementation of `deriveGrammarSites` rather than guessed. That inventory is the reason this was affordable at all: it forced every new class to be proven instead of shipping unpinned.
- EVIDENCE: 4 mutants, all killed. A2 is the exact reported bug — re-adding the original core-triple gate reddens the `1.2.3-rc.1` case at `session_record_versions_test.go:1038`. B (narrow the pattern back) reddens 5 accept cases; C (delete the migration gate) reddens 8 refuse cases, so widening is provably not the same as deleting. D (drop the member from `requireExactMembers`) reddens the absence case.
- ANOMALY: mutant D was GREEN on the first attempt. The absence case deleted `adapter_version` from `source_environment` only, and the surviving member in the sibling `target_environment` produced an unrelated extra-member refusal — the test passed for the wrong reason. Fixed by deleting from BOTH tuples and attributing the refusal by name. A refusal that fires is not evidence that YOUR clause fired.
- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/closed_shapes.go` (production), `grammar_inventory_test.go`, `member_shape_refusal_test.go`, `session_record_versions_test.go`, `testdata/constraint-enumeration.md`, `README.md`.
- STATUS: `internal/canonicaljson` 97.2% before and after, verified against a `git archive HEAD` baseline. gofmt, `go build ./...`, both cross-compile targets, `go vet ./...`, `go test ./... -count=1`, `-cover -count=1`, `-race -count=1`, tracecheck, `cataloggen -check` and all five fuzz gates at 100x exit 0.

### 2237 — A fault suite that only proves the call failed proves the cheap half

- FINDING (review round 2, blocking): seven fault boundaries were pinned at 2204, but only three carried a recovery half. Staged mode drift, both FIFO shard cases and BOTH quarantine-ENOSPC subtests asserted the failure state and stopped. Anything can fail; what a durability suite exists to establish is what a later run can still do. Both quarantine-ENOSPC cases are full-disk cases, so this was a direct miss against the front-loaded rule that full-disk and short-write cases must show the store is left recoverable.
- FINDING: `README.md` then described all seven as sharing four properties — digest path absent, no staged residue, nothing manufactured into quarantine, retry installs the declared bytes. Four of the seven did not carry all four, and for the existing-quarantine-denied case two of the four are FALSE BY DESIGN and the tests correctly assert the opposite: the corrupt object stays at the digest path and the candidate IS preserved in quarantine. The very next sentence stated that correct behavior, so the section contradicted itself across two adjacent sentences. This is the capability-claim-that-does-not-reproduce shape; a later contributor would read recovery-after-fault as pinned at boundaries where nothing reddens if it breaks.
- DECISION: took the stronger fix rather than narrowing the prose. Recovery halves added to all five under-covered cases plus the symlinked-shard case, and the README quantifier rewritten so the quarantine-move-denied outcome is stated SEPARATELY instead of folded into a list whose properties it contradicts.
- DECISION: the two quarantine-ENOSPC recoveries have a different SHAPE and the tests now say so at the case. A candidate that was never a valid object cannot install on retry, so its recovery is that it is preserved as evidence exactly as it would have been without the fault. A corrupt existing object is deliberately left at the digest path, so its recovery is what the NEXT run does with it still there.
- FINDING: that next run was entirely unpinned. It reaches `resolveExistingEntry` with a `blobMismatch` verdict, quarantines BOTH artifacts, frees the digest path, and a third run then installs. So a denied quarantine move defers the cleanup rather than stranding the digest path — worth knowing, and nothing established it before.
- EVIDENCE: 5 mutants, 5 killed, transcript in `TASK-260830-3qrfjp_recovery-mutation-evidence.log`. The three that matter each leave the FAULTED first half of the case passing and die only at a line added in this round. M5 moves the existing artifact but never reports its path (`object_store_fault_test.go:490`). M6 quarantines the mismatched candidate but never reports its path (`:434`). M4 classifies a genuine ABSENCE as an unsafe entry and is killed by all six retry halves at `:247`, `:291`, `:366` (both subtests), and `object_store_fault_unix_test.go:109`, `:147` — the absence-versus-failure-to-read distinction, seen from the recovery side.
- NOTE: the `assertQuarantineIsEmpty` added to the group-accessible-shard case is a state assertion backing a README claim, not a gate proof; that refusal returns before anything is staged, so no natural mutant can put something in quarantine there. Recorded rather than presented as mutation-tested.
