### 2204 — A write that did not finish is not a mismatch either, and it was being quarantined as one

- FINDING: `PutBlob` folded the staged-LENGTH comparison into the same condition as the two source-IDENTITY comparisons: `info.Size() < 0 || uint64(info.Size()) != actualSize || actualSize != expectedSize || actualDigest != expected`. The identity terms compare what the source produced against what the caller declared, which is the SPEC.md Section 3.2 quarantine trigger. The length term compares what the copy ACCEPTED against what the filesystem KEPT, which proves nothing about the source bytes at all. This is the exact write-side mirror of the read-side defect recorded at 2030 today, in the same function, and it survived that fix.
- FINDING: on a delayed-allocation filesystem (ext4, XFS, APFS) ENOSPC surfaces at writeback, not at the accepting write. So the disagreement is reachable with NO error raised on either side: `io.Copy` reports the full length, the hasher agrees with the declared digest, and only `fstat` disagrees.
- ROOT CAUSE of the consequence: a fragment was installed into quarantine and reported with `PutResult.Digest`/`Size` describing bytes no artifact on disk held — the digest of the full source attached to a truncated file. An incident record built from that result would name a digest that does not match the quarantined bytes. Second consequence: a full volume made the store write MORE data, into quarantine, on the volume whose exhaustion caused the fault.
- FIX: `internal/localstore/object_store.go:219` — the condition is split. A staged-length disagreement returns `ErrDurability` with no quarantine path and a zero `PutResult`; the fragment is discarded by the existing deferred cleanup. `README.md` now says "size or digest mismatches OF THE SOURCE BYTES" are quarantined.
- DECISION: write-side faults are driven through the PRE-EXISTING `storeOperations.syncFile` seam, not a new one. fsync is where a real ENOSPC is discovered, so a failure raised there and a length that changed by stat time are the two shapes the condition actually takes. No new production seam was added to the object store.
- FINDING: `PRAGMA max_page_count` pinned to the database's current page count makes SQLite raise a genuine `SQLITE_FULL` (code 13, "database or disk is full") from inside a transaction — no filesystem, no root, no RAM disk. The limit lives on the CONNECTION, not in the file (verified: reopening reports 4294967294), so the constraint vanishes when the failed open closes it and the recovery half of each case runs unconstrained. One test-only field, `projectionHooks.afterDatabaseOpen`, reaches that connection.
- FINDING: a full volume must NOT be classified as corruption. The corruption path quarantines the index and rebuilds it from scratch, which writes more data onto the volume that just ran out of room. It is correct today; nothing pinned it before.
- FINDING: the directory fixture at the digest path REACHED the regularity clause in `inspectExisting` but did not isolate it — remove the clause and `os.Open` succeeds, `io.Copy` fails EISDIR, the unreadable verdict still refuses. A FIFO at exactly mode 0600 isolates it and bounds availability: with the clause removed `PutBlob` HANGS on a pipe with no writer. Confirmed by mutant M4, which failed with "PutBlob did not return within 20s" rather than a wrong value.
- EVIDENCE: 9 mutants, 9 killed. M1 (delete the length term) installs a TRUNCATED file into the immutable namespace and returns `error = <nil>`. M2 narrows `!=` to `<`: the short case still passes and the over-long case admits — the class-coverage proof a delete-only mutant cannot give. M3 re-merges the branch and the refusal comes back as `ErrIntegrityMismatch` with a quarantined fragment. M7 wraps rebuild inserts as `ErrProjectionCorrupt`; M8 routes a failed rebuild through the quarantine path and reddens with "index-recovery holds 1 directories"; M9 drops the migration rollback and the NEXT open fails `SQLITE_BUSY` on the leaked transaction. Transcript: `TASK-260830-3qrfjp_mutation-evidence.log`.
- NOTE: real filesystem exhaustion is deliberately not driven. A RAM disk or loopback image is a machine-mutating, non-deterministic side effect and is refused by the repository's deterministic-test rule; the seam and the page limit reproduce the same error values on the same code paths.
- FINDING: the symlink dimension had a hole one level above the leaf. The pre-existing symlink test replaces the digest path, which `inspectExisting` classifies. Replacing the SHARD DIRECTORY is decided much earlier, by the owner-only child-tree walk, and mutant M6 shows what that walk is holding: with it replaced by a plain `MkdirAll`, `PutBlob` returns `error = <nil>` and installs the immutable object OUTSIDE the AX data root, through the symlink.
- SCOPE: `README.md`, `LOGBOOK.md`, `internal/localstore/object_store.go`, `internal/localstore/projection.go`, `internal/localstore/projection_test.go`, `internal/localstore/object_store_fault_test.go`, `internal/localstore/object_store_fault_unix_test.go`, `internal/localstore/projection_fault_test.go`
- STATUS: TASK-260830-3qrfjp on `task-board/story/STORY-260902-2ni6i3`, uncommitted candidate at checkpoint `c067365`; `internal/localstore` 85.6% -> 86.6% and 80 -> 90 tests, every other package unchanged; all 18 configured spawn validation commands exit 0, including `go test ./... -race -count=1`, five seeded fuzz targets, both cross-compile targets and global tracecheck; handed off to review.

### 2122 — A byte bound measured on Go's escaped encoding is a bound nobody declared

- ROOT CAUSE: `validateSessionLaunchPlan` and `internal/config`'s `validateExtensions` both measured a declared 65,536-byte bound with `len(json.Marshal(value))`. `encoding/json` rewrites each of the single bytes `<`, `>` and `&` into a six-character escape, and U+2028/U+2029 from three canonical bytes into six, so the bound in force was up to 6x tighter than the pinned one and was a property of `encoding/json` rather than of the specification. It failed closed — nothing oversized was admitted — but a CONFORMING document was refused, and the third site, `validateExtensionsObject`, already measured canonical bytes. One repository carried both the defect and its fix.
- FINDING: `SetEscapeHTML(false)` alone does NOT fix it. `encoding/json` escapes U+2028 and U+2029 regardless of that setting, so only the RFC 8785 transform makes the measurement independent of the encoder. Probed directly: `["<"]` is 5 canonical bytes and 10 marshalled; `[U+2028]` is 7 canonical and 10 marshalled.
- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/declared_byte_bounds.go` — `CanonicalByteLength` (encode with `SetEscapeHTML(false)`, then `Canonicalize`) is the one measurement, and `canonicalByteBound(name, value, maximum)` is the one refusal gate. `closed_shapes.go:284` and `closed_shapes.go:1794` call the gate; `internal/config/validation.go:763` calls the measurement. `internal/config` now imports `internal/canonicaljson`; no cycle, canonicaljson imports only `catalog` and `scalar`.
- FINDING: `canonicalByteBound`'s signature (`name string` + `maximum int`) matches the derived bound-helper shape in `declared_bounds_test.go`, so both byte bounds became first-class derived obligations automatically. Two `boundaryConstraintCase` entries now claim them, and changing either literal reddens the derivation gate as well as the new tests. Sharing the gate was chosen for deduplication; wiring into the derived-obligation inventory was a free consequence of the existing scanner, not a hand-registered one.
- DECISION: folding both call sites onto `canonicalByteBound` instead of leaving `if err != nil { ... }` inline at each. The inline shape measured 64 uncovered statements in `internal/canonicaljson` against a baseline of 63 (97.143% vs 97.179%), because each call site carried its own unreachable measure-error guard. The shared gate carries one, and the final tree is 63 uncovered / 97.185% — coverage slightly above baseline rather than below it.
- EVIDENCE: 8 mutants, 8 killed. M1 (`CanonicalByteLength` returns the marshalled length) reddens all four canonicaljson tests and both config tests. M2-M5 narrow and widen each canonicaljson bound by one; M6-M7 do the same to `maxConfigExtensionBytes`; M8 restores the escaped measurement at the config site alone. Narrow/widen mutants matter here because a delete-only mutant proves the gate exists and says nothing about where it sits. Transcript: `BUG-260902-3v8ck5_mutation-sweep.log`.
- NOTE: the tests are built from characters whose two encodings differ, plus one encoding-neutral ASCII control row that shares the same fixture machinery. Every row asserts the accepted at-limit document is OVER the bound when measured the escaped way — that assertion, not the acceptance, is what kills the mutant. Fixture sizes are verified through `Canonicalize`, not through the helper under test, and the limit is a local SPEC literal, never `maxConfigExtensionBytes`.
- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/declared_byte_bounds.go`, `internal/canonicaljson/closed_shapes.go`, `internal/canonicaljson/canonical_byte_bound_measurement_test.go`, `internal/canonicaljson/boundary_constraints_test.go`, `internal/canonicaljson/refusal_guards_test.go`, `internal/config/validation.go`, `internal/config/extension_canonical_byte_bound_test.go`
- STATUS: BUG-260902-3v8ck5 on `task-board/story/STORY-260902-2rngvr`, uncommitted candidate at checkpoint `f20aeec`; canonicaljson 97.2% and every other package unchanged against a detached-worktree baseline at the same commit; `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`, `gofmt -l internal/`, `go test ./... -cover -count=1`, global tracecheck, `go generate ./internal/catalog`, `cataloggen -check` and all five seeded fuzz targets exit 0; handed off to review.

### 2030 — A read that did not finish is not a mismatch, and quarantine cannot tell the difference on its own

- FINDING: `PutBlob` routed EVERY non-`ErrUnsafeOwnership` inspection error to `quarantineExisting`. `inspectExisting` returned raw `os.Open`, `io.Copy` and `Close` errors, so at the decision point an EMFILE or a transient I/O error was indistinguishable from a hash mismatch. A flaky read on the existing digest-path entry permanently moved a VALID immutable object out of the namespace. SPEC.md:819-820 authorizes quarantine for "a hash mismatch or representation disagreement" only, and a read that never completed proves neither.
- FINDING: the projection already got this right — `scanAuthoritativeBlobs` classified a failed read as `sourceFailure` and refused. The two paths disagreed, and the store side is the one that moves data. Nothing in the package made them agree; they were two independent open/copy/hash blocks that happened to be written by different hands.
- DECISION: extracted `verifyBlobContent` in `internal/localstore/blob_inspection.go` as the single owner of "read this digest-path entry and report what that proved", returning a `blobVerdict` whose zero value `blobUnproven` is deliberately not a proof. Both `inspectExisting` and `scanAuthoritativeBlobs` now consult it; each still maps the verdict onto its own consequence, so the projection's two `projectionRefusal` call sites stay in `projection.go` where the derived refusal inventory can still demand a negative path for them.
- DECISION: `blobVerdict.quarantineWarranted()` is the one predicate authorizing a move, and only `blobMismatch` satisfies it. `resolveExistingEntry` funnels BOTH the first-attempt and the raced post-rename branches, which previously carried two hand-copied classification blocks that could drift apart independently.
- DECISION: `storeOperations.openExisting` and `projectionHooks.openBlob` are injected read seams. Without them the transient-failure path needs real descriptor exhaustion or media failure, which is why the original auditor could only argue the defect rather than reproduce it.
- EVIDENCE: 5 mutants, 5 killed; baseline and restored tree both exit 0. Collapsing the classification back (delete the `blobUnreadable` branch, gate quarantine on `verdict == blobUnsafe`) reddens 3 of 4 injected-failure subtests plus the store/projection agreement assertion. Widening `quarantineWarranted` to "any verdict that is not a match" reddens the three pre-existing unsafe-entry tests — the unsafe entry starts getting moved. Narrowing it to `false` and narrowing the mismatch comparison to size-only both redden `TestPutBlobQuarantinesBothInputsWhenExistingDigestPathIsCorrupt`; the size-only mutant is the one that proves the same-size digest disagreement is a real bound, not a delete-only artifact. Making `scanAuthoritativeBlobs` ignore `hooks.openBlob` reddens the agreement test alone, which is what keeps that test from passing on a seam the projection never consults. Evidence: `BUG-260902-1qsdag_mutant-evidence-final.log`.
- NOTE: the agreement is asserted, not assumed. `TestObjectStoreAndProjectionAgreeOnIncompleteReadVersusProvenMismatch` drives `PutBlob` and `openProjection` over the same two on-disk conditions and fails if their proven-mismatch classification differs, if the projection ever moves a durable object, or if the store moves one without a completed disagreeing read.
- SCOPE: `README.md`, `LOGBOOK.md`, `internal/localstore/blob_inspection.go`, `internal/localstore/blob_inspection_test.go`, `internal/localstore/object_store.go`, `internal/localstore/projection.go`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json`, `internal/traceability/traceability.go`
