### 2240 — A pinned vocabulary set does not pin the JSON path that reaches it

- FINDING: `testdata/closed-vocabularies.md` pins the admitted set of all 47 production vocabulary call sites and kills any widening mutant at the source. It says nothing about WHICH member each gate is reached from. A validator that read a Lease Record `reason` through the `session.quiescing` reason gate would leave every pinned row byte-identical while admitting `stop` on a lease. The pin is over the source, the defect is over the wiring.
- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/record_conformance_test.go` walks every closed-vocabulary position of every identity fixture — 149 positions — and feeds each the union of every OTHER pinned vocabulary, 14,639 substitutions, requiring refusal outside that position's own declared set. Positions are found by VALUE, not by a member-name list, so a new enum in a new record is swept without being registered anywhere.
- ANOMALY, and the reason the first version of this sweep was worthless: keying the declared set by member NAME let the widening mutant through. `reason` is declared twice — Lease Record `{create, graceful_takeover, force_takeover, recovery}` and `session.quiescing` `{graceful_takeover, stop, checkpoint}` — so the member-name union already contained `stop` and the sweep never substituted it. Mutant M3 SURVIVED the first run. Same shape for `kind` (session vs Transfer Manifest) and `materialization_policy` (git vs managed_tree member).
- FIX: the declared set is now the single pinned row whose member AND admitted values contain the fixture's own value, with candidates deduplicated by admitted set so six identical `execution_profile` rows count as one vocabulary. The four genuinely ambiguous positions carry a reviewed resolution that must remain one of the candidate rows, so a widened production vocabulary invalidates the resolution instead of being absorbed by it, and a stale resolution naming no position is reported.
- NOTE: a position whose value matches NO row for its member is an exact-literal site, not a vocabulary — `clone.failed` pins `phase=checkpoint` while `clone.target_validation_failed` carries a six-member `phase` enum. It is swept as a single-value set, which is the stronger bound, rather than skipped. Free strings that coincidentally hold a pinned value (a manifest entry whose `path` is literally `file`) fall out because no pinned row declares their member.
- EVIDENCE: `TASK-260830-uqnwmi_mutation-evidence.log`. M3 survives the member-name-keyed sweep and dies to the value-keyed one, same fixture, same substitution set.
- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/record_conformance_test.go`, `README.md`
- STATUS: Resolved; TASK-260830-uqnwmi handed to review.

### 2235 — Absence and grammar are different clauses, and requireExactMembers eats the first one

- FINDING: mutating `validateCommonRecordEnvelope` to skip its `created_by_host_id` check when the member is ABSENT survives both the new Section 10.1 provenance sweep and the whole existing package suite. Every Section 10.1 validator calls `requireExactMembers` with its closed member list BEFORE `validateIdentityRecordEnvelope`, so an object missing the member is refused as an incomplete member set and the mutated branch is never executed at either identity production entry. Equivalent mutant, not an evidence gap.
- FINDING, the reachable half, and it was NOT covered: mutating the same check from `requireUUIDv7` to `requireString` also survived the first draft of the sweep, because the sweep's malformed case used an empty string and `requireString` accepts it. A UUIDv4 stamped into a UUIDv7 member is well-formed JSON, well-formed UUID, and names no host — exactly the shape a forged or copied provenance stamp takes.
- FIX: envelope mutations now include a right-typed / wrong-grammar case per member — UUIDv4 and a non-UUID string for the UUIDv7 members, an impossible calendar date for `created_at`, a non-reverse-DNS key for `extensions`. The mutant dies.
- DECISION: the absent case is kept but its refusal REASON is pinned to `is missing required member "<member>"`, so it records which gate actually caught it instead of counting as evidence for a clause it never reached. Subsumption named and pinned rather than assumed, per the leaf's front-loaded DoD.
- NOTE: this is the general shape behind the DoD rule "supply a COMPLETE valid member set and violate exactly one thing". A negative case that deletes a member short-circuits on the member-set gate and proves nothing about the typed gate it is named after.
- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/record_conformance_test.go`
- STATUS: Resolved.

### 2110 — A specification's controlled vocabulary is a mapping, not a reading

- FINDING: `core_records.go` validated Session Event `predecessors` with `validateSortedUniqueDigests`, so a duplicate predecessor was **refused**. Pinned Section 5.2 (SPEC.md:1728) declares "`predecessors` as a **sorted** array of one or more record/event digests". Section 1.6 (SPEC.md:323) defines `sorted unique T[n..m]` as the COMPOUND phrase meaning "bytewise canonical ordering **and no duplicate**", and the document applies that phrase systematically — `event_heads`, `manifest_ids`, `evidence_ids`, `object_ids`, `accepted_risks`, `sanitized_remote_urls`, `agent_project_config_paths` — while using bare `sorted` for `predecessors` alone. Reading the two as synonyms invented a refusal. This was the FIFTH invented constraint on this leaf; the previous four were each fixed one at a time.
- FINDING, found by the new gate rather than by review: the SAME error sat in `validateWorkspaceGroupRecord`. Section 2 (SPEC.md:2146) declares "Members are sorted by `workspace_id`, and no two members may have an equal or case-colliding `group_relative_path`" — the declared uniqueness is on `group_relative_path`, never on `workspace_id`. Production refused two members sharing a `workspace_id`. Repaired, and the admission is now pinned by an accepting case at both identity entries.
- ROOT CAUSE, one level up from "read the spec more carefully": the document uses a CONTROLLED VOCABULARY for array constraints and the implementation treated one of its phrases as a synonym of another. Judgement is the failure mode, so judgement had to leave the loop.
- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/array_order_inventory_test.go` plus `testdata/array-order-constraints.md`. Ordering sites are derived structurally — a comparison between a value derived from the ranged element and a value bound to an EARLIER element, either carried forward or indexed at an offset from the loop key — so no rename hides one. Strength is read off the comparison OPERATOR (`<=` refuses duplicates, `<` admits them), and the member name is derived by tracing the ranged collection back to the string literal that named it, through helper parameters and across call sites. Order and uniqueness are pinned as SEPARATE factors with their own SPEC lines, because the document frequently declares them at different lines; `sorted unique` must cite a uniqueness declaration and bare `sorted` must cite none. A strengthened validator then cannot be written down consistently.
- NOTE: the gate proves its own coverage three ways rather than one. A site whose member cannot be traced is REPORTED, which is what a new ordering helper with no call site looks like — an unpinned rule that contributes no row and would otherwise slip past a sequence comparison entirely. Every production refusal whose message speaks about order must belong to a function carrying a derived site, which catches a check written in a shape the tracer does not model. And the mapping is a pure function with its own negative proof.
- NOTE, a survivor worth recording: the first version of that negative proof asserted only "some problem was reported". Narrowing the missing-uniqueness-citation clause SURVIVED, because the neighbouring default branch reported a different problem for the same row. Each case now pins the exact message it must produce, and a further mutant confirmed the matcher itself is exact. An isolated case must pin its own clause, not the fact that something complained.
- DISCLOSURE, not repaired here: `WorkspaceSnapshot.members` and `GitIndex.entries` enforce uniqueness the pinned document declares nowhere — an equal `workspace_id` and an equal `(path, stage)` pair respectively. Both sit in `closed_shapes.go`, in sections owned by `TASK-260830-uqnwmi`, inside a reviewed and accepted candidate. They are recorded as disclosed strengthenings with their exact undeclared refusal and routed, and the disclosed set is asserted exactly against the artifact in both directions so a fourth cannot appear silently.
- EVIDENCE: 26 mutants, 26 killed, 0 survivors, guarded baseline green before and after. Twelve flip an ordering operator at every derived site in the package (six strengthenings, six widenings), five attack the provenance checker itself, five edit the pinned artifact, and two add a second ordering helper and an order refusal in an unmodelled shape as new production files.
- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/`, `internal/traceability/`, `README.md`, `LOGBOOK.md`
- STATUS: Resolved; TASK-260830-1tax26 handed to review as revision 7.


### 1730 — A mutant grammar decides what a sweep can find; two shapes had none

- FINDING: two live gates in `internal/canonicaljson/core_records.go` were proven in one direction only, and review reproduced both end to end against the whole configured gate set. Narrowing `requiresError != errorPresent` to `requiresError && !errorPresent` made `ValidateObservationEvent` **return nil and attest** a `success` Observation Event carrying `"error_code":"boom"`. Narrowing `epoch != 1` to `epoch >= 3` made `CalculateObjectIdentity` **attest** an epoch-2 Lease Record with a null `predecessor_lease_id`. `go test ./...`, `tracecheck` and all four seeded fuzz targets stayed green through both.
- ROOT CAUSE, one level up from "we missed a case": the previous three sweeps rewrote comparison OPERATORS and deleted guards. Neither operation can express either shape. A boolean-valued `!=` has no operator worth rewriting — the mutant is a split of the identity into its two single-sided halves. And `epoch != 1` narrowed to `epoch > 1` is an EQUIVALENT mutant in a positive-integer domain, so an operator sweep reports a kill and says nothing; only a mutant carrying a different LITERAL (`>= K+2`, `!= K+1`) can move the boundary. A sweep proves exactly as much as its grammar can say.
- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/coupling_derivation_test.go` derives both subjects from the production sources. Boolean-valued is decided from the source — a negation, a nested comparison, a short-circuit, or an identifier the enclosing function binds from a package function whose declared result at that position is `bool` — so the coupling set is not a list. Integer-literal comparisons need no type inference at all: Go refuses to compile a comparison between an untyped integer constant and a non-numeric operand, so the compiler has already established the other side. `coupling_proofs_test.go` obliges two single-sided proofs per coupling (the pair chosen by the operator) and one proof at each value where a literal comparison flips (read off the operator), and asserts obligations equal proofs in both directions.
- NOTE: the derived key carries the literal, so moving a literal fails the gate BEFORE any case runs. Moving `epoch != 1` to `epoch != 5` was verified to redden the coverage assertion rather than merely one case.
- DECISION: the Observation result/error and tombstone couplings are driven for EVERY value the production vocabulary admits, resolved through the same derivation the closed-vocabulary inventory uses, rather than for one hand-picked value. Admit by derivation, refuse the complement.
- DECISION: three boundary values are unreachable and declared, not dropped — a zero `sequence` and a zero `epoch` are refused by the positive-integer gate first. Each names its subsuming refusal and a test that must exist. The one function whose comparisons run at package initialization over the pinned catalog is exempted by name, and the exemption asserts from the sources that every caller of it is a package-level initializer that panics.
- EVIDENCE: 40 mutants generated from that same derivation — both single-direction narrowings of every derived coupling, and literal shifts an operator grammar cannot express — 40 killed, 0 survivors, green baseline before and after. Five self-checks confirm the gates can fail: a deleted coupling proof, a deleted boundary proof, a duplicated claim, a moved production literal, and a narrowed derivation each redden the suite.
- SCOPE, stated rather than implied: the literal-boundary gate covers `core_records.go`, this leaf's file. `canonical.go` and `closed_shapes.go` remain owned by the sibling `TASK-260830-uqnwmi`. The presence-coupling gate is package-wide, because eight sites is small enough that scoping it would have left the two `validateGitFeatures` couplings — a known instance of the same class — unproven.
- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/`, `README.md`, `LOGBOOK.md`
- STATUS: Resolved; TASK-260830-1tax26 handed to review as revision 6.


### 1620 — endpoint option injection closed; ssh_args gate never read the endpoint
- ROOT CAUSE: `validateMesh` (`internal/config/validation.go:449`) bounded `mesh.peers[].endpoint` only with `validatePrintableCharacters(…, 1, 1024)`. The Section 6.3 bypass gate reads `ssh_args` and nothing else, and SPEC.md:2494 hands `ssh(1)` the endpoint as an atomic argv value — but an atomic argv value is not a destination, because `ssh(1)` reads its destination positionally through getopt.
- FINDING: reproduced at the production `loadConfigDocument` entry with a 34-shape hostile-endpoint probe. Against the pre-fix base (an archive of checkpoint `10aaa16`) 32 of 34 loaded with `err=nil`, including `endpoint = "-oStrictHostKeyChecking=no"`, `"-oUserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null"`, `"-F/attacker/config"`, `"-J jump.example"`, `"--"`, and `"peer.example -oStrictHostKeyChecking=no"`. The first two are exactly the bypass Section 6.3 requires refusing, reached through a field the ssh_args gate never inspects. Only the tab and newline shapes were already refused, one clause earlier by the printable-character bound. After the fix all 34 are refused and every option-shaped one names the `option-like value` clause. Probe logs: `.temp/BUG-260902-4ajzyz/audit-probe-before-01.log` and `-after-01.log`.
- FIX: `internal/config/endpoint.go` admits the field against a closed `[user@]host[:port]` grammar — leading `-` and any whitespace refused first by name, then a 1-64 character login name, LDH DNS labels or a bracketed IPv6 literal, and a decimal port in 1-65535. Wired as one new refusal site at `validation.go:453`, clause `mesh.peers[N].endpoint <reason>`.
- DECISION: the grammar is deliberately narrower than every destination `ssh(1)` accepts (no bare IPv6, no trailing dot, no `%zone`, LDH labels only). Fail-closed is the point: widening it is a reviewed change, not an accident. A bare IPv6 literal is refused specifically because its colons are indistinguishable from the port separator.
- FINDING: the dangling `-o` the audit reported as admitted was already closed by BUG-260902-beqfwr — `admitSSHArguments` returns `sshRefusalMissingFlagValue` for it today. Verified by probe, then pinned per declared value-taking letter (`-i -l -p -o`, alone, grouped as `-q<letter>`, and as a trailing argument) rather than left as the two incidental cases it had.
- FINDING: the disclosed short-flag asymmetry is real and is what let `-A`, `-E` and `-L` mutants pass green: `TestLoadRefusesEverySSHShortOptionOutsideThePermittedTables` skips whatever production permits, so a mutant that permits a letter deletes its own subtest. Closed with the both-directions key-set pin `permittedSSHOptionSamples` already gave the `-o` registry, plus named assertions for the capability-widening letters `-A -E -L -R -D -W -w -S -O`.
- REGRESSION: `schema_test.go`'s endpoint bound used `strings.Repeat("界", 1024)`, which the grammar refuses; rewritten as `strings.Repeat("a.", 511) + "ab"` so the 1024-character bound stays reachable through the grammar and is still proved at 1024 accepted / 1025 refused.
- SCOPE: `internal/config/endpoint.go` (new), `internal/config/endpoint_admission_test.go` (new), `internal/config/validation.go`, `internal/config/ssh_admission_test.go`, `internal/config/schema_test.go`, `README.md`.
- FINDING: `-ivan@peer.example` is the shape that proves the leading-hyphen clause is load-bearing rather than redundant with the grammar. The `[user@]host[:port]` rules alone admit it, because `-` is a legal login-name byte, so only the leading-hyphen clause refuses it.
- REGRESSION: review found that claim overclaimed as shipped. None of the eight cases in `TestLoadRefusesOptionLikeMeshEndpoints` carries an `@`, so every one of them is *also* refused by the host clause once the hyphen clause is gone; they pinned the clause's reason and its ordering, never its coverage class, and the `-ivan@peer.example` proof lived only in a `.temp` probe that never reached the committed tests. A single-clause narrowing to `strings.HasPrefix(endpoint, "-") && !strings.Contains(endpoint, "@")` therefore kept the whole `internal/config` suite green while admitting `-ivan@peer.example`, which `ssh(1)` parses as `-i` with identity file `van@peer.example` — a live injection.
- FIX: `TestLoadRefusesOptionShapedEndpointsTheGrammarWouldAdmit` commits seven `@`-bearing option-shaped endpoints, each asserted refused *and* admitted once its leading `-` is removed. All seven redden under the narrowing mutant (M04).
- DECISION: the isolation rule is now structural for the whole endpoint grammar, not applied only to the two shapes review named. Every refusal case in `endpointGrammarCases` carries a `neighbour` — the same endpoint with only the named violation removed — that must be admitted, so a case cannot come to rest on a clause other than the one it names; `TestEveryRefusedMeshEndpointCaseDeclaresAnIsolatingNeighbour` reddens if a later case is added without one.
- FINDING: the whitespace clause is the one clause that cannot be pinned by admission — no whitespace-carrying endpoint is spellable by a login name or a DNS label, so the grammar refuses the same inputs with a different reason. It is pinned by its named reason and its ordering ahead of the grammar instead, and the entry says so rather than claiming an admission gap it does not close.
- DECISION: `endpoint = "peer.example:022"` is admitted; a leading-zero port reads as decimal 22 and carries no option or whitespace. Review flagged that the case named `padded port` pinned only the 1..5-byte length bound, so the name overclaimed. `:022` is now pinned as admitted reviewed behaviour, and the length bound gets cases that rest on it alone (`:000022` and `:0000022`, decimal 22 and inside the numeric bound, so only `len(port) > 5` refuses them).
- FINDING: bare-IPv6-with-port was unpinned. Widening `admitEndpointHost` to accept anything `net.ParseIP` accepts survived the old suite, because the only bare-IPv6 case carried no port and never reached the widened branch; `2001:db8::1:2222` and `::1:22` now close it (M17).
- EVIDENCE: 28 single-clause mutants over `endpoint.go`, `sshargs.go` and the `validateMesh` wiring, 26 killed and 2 proven equivalent. Each killed mutant reddens only its own cases: M01-M04 and M24 (leading-hyphen deleted, narrowed to `-o`, narrowed to `--`, carved out for `@`, and reordered behind whitespace) redden the option-shaped tests; M17 (host widened to anything `net.ParseIP` accepts) reddens the two bare-IPv6-with-port cases; M18 (port length upper bound dropped) reddens `port over length bound` alone; M26/M27 (`sshPermittedFlags` gains `-A`, `sshPermittedValueFlags` gains `-L`) redden the key-set pin and the named capability-widening refusals. Mutants that delete a clause and leave its import unused are reported as INVALID-BUILD rather than counted as kills. Deleting `host == ""` leaves `strings.Split("", ".")` returning one empty label that `admitEndpointHostLabel` refuses with the same reason; dropping `len(port) < 1` leaves the empty port at `number == 0`, which `number >= 1` refuses with the same reason. Both were confirmed equivalent by a differential dump of `admitMeshEndpoint` over a generated corpus, byte-identical to baseline. Harness and logs: `.temp/BUG-260902-4ajzyz/mutants2.py`, `mutants-04.log`, `equiv-*.txt`. That set was *not* exhaustive — see the boundary-adjacency regression below.
- REGRESSION: rev2 review found one live surviving mutant, and it was introduced by the previous cycle: narrowing `admitEndpointPort` from `len(port) > 5` to `> 6` kept the whole suite green while admitting `peer.example:000022`. The case that owned the clause, `port over length bound`, uses a *seven*-byte port, which stays refused under the narrowed bound — so it pinned the bound to somewhere in [5,6] rather than to 5. The reviewed baseline patch of BUG-260902-3c7ovg had a six-byte `padded port` case that killed this mutant exactly, and rev2 replaced it. The earlier claim that both surviving mutants were equivalent was true of the 28 mutants that cycle ran, but that set contained only the *deletion* of this bound, never its one-step narrowing, and the entry above is corrected to say so. No exposure: the strings newly admitted are six-byte zero-padded ports whose decimal value is already inside 1..65535 and which `ssh(1)` resolves identically. The defect was in the evidence, not the gate.
- DECISION: boundary adjacency is now structural, not a per-case convention — this is the third time in this Story a bound proved at a distant value left its edge unpinned. `endpointBoundEdges` names every numeric bound the grammar declares and carries the last admitted value with the first refused one: user length 64/65 and 1/0, host label length 62-in-63/64 and 1/0, port length 5/6 and 1/0, port value 65535/65536 and 1/0. `TestEveryDeclaredMeshEndpointBoundIsPinnedAtItsEdge` drives both sides of each pair through the production Load entry, so a bound that loses its adjacent case reddens. The endpoint's own 1..1024 character bound stays owned by `schema_test.go`, already proved at 1024/1025.
- FINDING: audited every other bound in the table for the same shape; the port length upper bound was the only one proved at a distance. One bound cannot be pinned by adjacency at all and is recorded rather than papered over: `admitEndpointHost`'s bracketed `len(host) < 3` excludes exactly the string `[]`, and `net.ParseIP("")` is nil, so the sub-clause is unreachable and no admitted bracketed host exists at length 3 (the shortest is `[::]` at 4).
- EVIDENCE (rework): 18 further single-clause mutants run at this tree from an independent harness (`.temp/BUG-260902-4ajzyz/mutants3.py`, `mutants3b.py`; logs `mutants-rework-01.log`, `-02.log`), each reported with the exact leaf subtests it reddens. The reviewer's live survivor is now KILLED and reddens only the two cases that own the clause — `…BoundIsPinnedAtItsEdge/port_length_upper` and `…DeclaredMeshEndpointGrammar/port_at_length_bound`. Every bound was mutated in both directions where both are expressible; `M11` (label lower bound) kills by making production panic on an empty label, and is reported as such. `M20` (`len(port) < 1` dropped) survives and is equivalent by measurement, not argument: 0 of 9,120 corpus rows differ, while the same corpus separates the `> 5` -> `> 6` narrowing on 178 rows, so it is discriminating rather than blind. Dumps: `equiv-rework-baseline.txt`, `-m19.txt`, `-m20.txt`.
- SCOPE (review cycle): `internal/config/endpoint_admission_test.go`, `LOGBOOK.md`, `README.md`. No production change in either review cycle — review confirmed `endpoint.go` correct against 68 hostile shapes on all three schema versions and again against 171 loads on rev2; both defects were in the evidence, not the gate.
- EVIDENCE (reapply): the two clauses the acceptance criteria single out were re-proved at THIS tree rather than carried on the previous cycle's word. Narrowing `admitEndpointPort` from `len(port) > 5` to `> 6` is KILLED and reddens exactly the two leaf subtests that own it — `…BoundIsPinnedAtItsEdge/port_length_upper` and `…DeclaredMeshEndpointGrammar/port_at_length_bound` — so the bound still pins to its limit and not to a range. Adding `'N'` to `sshPermittedFlags`, a letter no named capability-widening assertion covers, is KILLED by the key-set pin alone (`ssh_admission_test.go:364: permitted flag -N has no declared sample`) and by nothing else in the package, which is the asymmetry that pin exists to close. Both mutants were reverted and the sources verified byte-identical by blob hash afterwards. Logs: `.temp/BUG-260902-4ynj93/mutant-m1-portbound.log`, `mutant-m2-flagN.log`.
- REAPPLY: reapplied as BUG-260902-4ynj93 on `task-board/story/STORY-260902-9s0i85` from trunk checkpoint `139a691`, one commit past it. The accepted patch applied at exact context everywhere except `LOGBOOK.md`, whose day block had gained five entries since the accepted tree; the entry above is placed in descending-timestamp order between 1730 and 1400 rather than at the top of the day, and nothing in it is rewritten. `internal/config/endpoint.go`, `endpoint_admission_test.go`, `schema_test.go`, `ssh_admission_test.go` and `validation.go` are byte-identical to the accepted tree, proved by blob hash against the patch post-image index lines (`d5e59d9 56c9368 e37b9cb 504894f e7505f9`). `README.md` is the one reported deviation: its trunk base is `3a0d0f7` against the patch's `014f8f5`, so the post-image blob cannot match, but the delta this leaf introduces is exactly the accepted +25 lines and nothing else — the difference is pre-existing trunk content elsewhere in the file, absorbed nowhere.
