### 1400 — Pinning what a gate is GIVEN does not pin what the gate DOES with it

- FINDING: the 47-row closed-vocabulary pin from the previous entry works, and it is still not the admitted set. The admitted set is `member list INTERSECT matcher semantics`, and the pin covers only the first factor. Case-folding the comparison inside `requireEnum` made `CalculateObjectIdentity` **accept and attest** a Lease Record whose `reason` was `CREATE`; trimming made it attest `" create "`; relaxing `requireExactString` from equality to a prefix test made it attest a Checkpoint Record whose `status` was `validated_but_not_really`. `go test ./...`, all four seeded fuzz corpora, `tracecheck`, the 47-row pin AND the 97.1% refusal-coverage gate stayed green through every one of them, because the pinned rows still match the source and the refusal branch is still executed for whichever outside value a test happened to pick.
- ROOT CAUSE, one level deeper than the last three entries: an inventory pins the ARGUMENTS a gate is given. Nothing pinned the comparison. `requireEnum` is the single admission primitive behind all 47 pinned call sites and `requireExactString` behind 32 more that no inventory covered at all, so one edit to either moved 74 call sites at once, invisibly.
- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/string_admission_test.go`. Deciders are derived from the sources — a function that reads a named member out of a candidate object and decides its admission from a string parameter of its own — deliberately broad about HOW it compares, so `EqualFold` or `HasPrefix` keeps a decider inside its own gate instead of making it vanish. Forwarders are not pinned: the walk visits the inner call and resolves member and values in the SAME frame chain out through the forwarder's own callers, so a wrapper contributes one site per concrete caller rather than a Cartesian product. Sites bind to fixture positions by BEHAVIOUR — a site binds only where every declared member is admitted — which both disambiguates two `kind` vocabularies and makes a NARROWED matcher fail, since a matcher that drops a member binds nowhere. Each bound position is then attacked at both identity entries with a family built from the admitted value itself: three case folds, four whitespace forms, a proper prefix, a proper suffix, two proper superstrings, an embedded NUL. 11/11 matcher mutants killed.
- FINDING, same shape for grammars: widening `observationNamePattern` from `{1,7}` to `{1,8}` segments, to `{0,7}` so a bare single-segment name is admitted, and adding a hyphen to its character class each survived the whole gate set, as did `boardLogicalIDPattern` at `{0,127}` → `{0,128}`.
- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/grammar_inventory_test.go` derives every `regexp.MustCompile` in the package with its POLARITY derived from use, pins each against a reference written independently of the source, derives one obligation per anchor, character class, counted bound and one-or-more quantifier FROM THE REFERENCE, and requires a witness that the reference refuses and that the reference with exactly that dimension widened admits — so a witness that could not fail against a widened pattern is rejected before it is trusted. 71 derived mutants + 4 reviewer-named + 3 controls, all killed.
- NOTE, the sharpest lesson of the round: **a single witness per character class proves nothing about a narrower widening.** A control mutant widening `providerIdentityKeyPattern` to admit uppercase SURVIVED the witness table, because the mechanical widening the table verifies against is maximal (`any character`) while the mutant added only `A-Z`. The fix is not more witnesses: it is a corpus. Every one-character neighbour of the value the fixture already carries — each position substituted by each of twelve character families, each family inserted at each boundary, each position deleted — is driven through the production entry. Nothing about it is hand-written, and the template is derived by probing rather than wired.
- FINDING, invented-constraint class #4, caught by a gate this time: three grammars carried a "pinned SPEC declaration" for a document that states the rule only in prose. `TestEveryPinnedGrammarReferenceQuotesItsSpecDeclaration` requires the reference to appear verbatim in its own quoted declaration or to disclose the composition, and named `lowerSnakePattern`, `mediaTypePattern` and `windowsAbsolutePathPattern`. All three are now recorded as implementation-defined with the prose quoted rather than cited.
- FINDING: `opaque_identity` was an empty map in the Provider Identity fixture, so `providerIdentityKeyPattern` was reachable from NO fixture and proven by no derived sweep. An empty open map is a silent hole in every fixture-walking gate. The fixture now carries one member, and `closedObjectMemberPaths` learned the open-map set (`extensions`, `env_literals`, `opaque_identity`) with the clause for each quoted, since their keys are data rather than schema members.
- DECISION: gates that cannot fail when weakened were treated as defects, not as style. Two of eleven self-attack mutants survived their first run — deleting a dimension from the adversarial family, and disabling the vocabulary provenance check — and both forced a fix: the family has a pinned dimension list, the provenance check is a pure function driven against rows wrong in each way it claims to detect, and the neighbour generator's corpus shape is asserted exactly.
- NOTE: no production Go source changed in this revision. The review had already probed the three survivors and found production CORRECT; what was missing was a gate that fails when the comparison behind them is relaxed. A revision that fixes only tests is the honest response to a verdict about unpinned semantics.
- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/`, `README.md`, `LOGBOOK.md`
- STATUS: Resolved; TASK-260830-1tax26 handed to review as revision 5.


### 1353 — ssh_args admission derived from a declared grammar, not a name blacklist
- ROOT CAUSE: `sshHostAuthenticationBypass` (`internal/config/validation.go:846`, called from `:465`) matched three option names, which cannot satisfy SPEC.md 6.3's "or an equivalent host-authentication bypass"; it inspected only whole `-o` tokens and matched only `no|off`.
- FINDING: reproduced against OpenSSH 10.2p1 on this host — `ssh -G -vo StrictHostKeyChecking=no` prints `stricthostkeychecking false` (grouped short flags were never seen), `-o StrictHostKeyChecking=false` and `=NO` also print `false` (live aliases, case-folded), and `-F`, `ProxyCommand`, `KnownHostsCommand`, `Include`, `PermitLocalCommand`+`LocalCommand` were unknown to the filter entirely.
- FIX: `internal/config/sshargs.go` declares the ssh(1) short-option arity transcribed from the OpenSSH usage text, the short options AX admits (`4 6 C T a q v`, `i l p o`), and an `-o` registry with per-option value rules; `admitSSHArguments` walks argv with getopt semantics and refuses the complement, so an undeclared option fails closed. One refusal call site remains at `validation.go:465`, now carrying a closed reason vocabulary in the clause.
- DECISION: `StrictHostKeyChecking` declared with only the enforcing spelling `yes`, so `no`/`off`/`false`/`accept-new` and anything OpenSSH adds later are refused without listing aliases. Host-authentication option names are still declared in the registry — not to widen admission, but so their refusal reports the 6.3 clause instead of an unknown-name clause.
- FINDING: `-i` consuming the next argv as a filename (`ssh -G -i -oProxyCommand=id` sets no proxycommand) is faithful OpenSSH behavior and is admitted deliberately; a token scanner that "found" `-oProxyCommand=` there would diverge from what ssh actually does.
- FINDING: a derived per-letter loop goes quiet under the mutant that permits that letter — the subtest simply stops existing. Cited bypasses need an explicitly named assertion alongside the derivation; M1 (permit `-F`) reddens only because of it.
- REGRESSION: two pre-existing bound tests in `internal/config/schema_test.go` used `"xxxx…"` filler as `ssh_args`; that is a bare operand and is now refused, so they were rewritten to `-i<path>` argv of identical byte lengths.
- SCOPE: `internal/config/sshargs.go` (new), `internal/config/ssh_admission_test.go` (new), `internal/config/validation.go`, `internal/config/refusal_test.go`, `internal/config/schema_test.go`, `README.md`.
- REAPPLY: this leaf was reprovisioned onto a fresh Story workspace after an empty-OID checkpoint; the accepted patch was reapplied rather than reimplemented, and every gate and mutant below was rerun in the new workspace instead of inherited from the earlier run. The five cited OpenSSH behaviours were reproduced again against the live OpenSSH 10.2p1 binary on this host.
- STATUS: BUG-260902-beqfwr on `task-board/story/STORY-260902-2230n7`, one commit past checkpoint `6f77719`; `internal/config` coverage 93.7% (measured at the checkpoint) -> 93.9%; `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`, `gofmt -l .`, `go test ./... -count=1`, `go test ./... -cover`, tracecheck, and the catalog `-check` gate all exit 0; ten single-clause mutants each redden their own cases; handed off to review.

