### 0500 — A vocabulary gate that refuses one outside value proves nothing about the admitted set

- FINDING: a derived mutation sweep that admitted **one extra member** at each of the 47 closed-vocabulary call sites in `internal/canonicaljson` survived **47 out of 47 times**. `go test ./...`, all four seeded fuzz corpora, `tracecheck`, the derived declared-bounds inventory and the derived refusal-guard coverage gate were all green while, for example, `validateLeaseRecord` admitted a fifth lease reason and `validateObservationEvent` admitted a sixth result.
- ROOT CAUSE: every vocabulary had a "refuses a value outside the vocabulary" negative, which proves the gate is REACHABLE, not that the admitted set is the DECLARED set. Coverage cannot separate the two: a widened gate still executes its refusal for whichever outside value the test picked. This is the same shape as the bounds finding one round earlier — an obligation that does not carry the literal cannot fail when the literal moves.
- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/closed_vocabularies_test.go` derives the vocabulary-admitting helpers from the sources (a function that yields the value it admitted and decides admission from its own variadic set, plus any wrapper forwarding that set to one), derives every call site with its member and admitted values in declaration order, and pins the result against a reviewed `testdata/closed-vocabularies.md` that cites the SPEC line declaring each vocabulary. Widening, narrowing, reordering, an unpinned call site and a deleted row all fail — at derivation, before any case runs. 47/47 mutants now killed.
- NOTE: the helper set is asserted complete, so a second copy of `requireEnum` reddens rather than quietly carrying unpinned vocabularies. Proven, not asserted: a duplicate helper in a new production file was added and the gate named it.
- FINDING, same sweep: three refusal guards in `core_records.go` were proven by one witness each where the guard has several disjuncts — the `opaque_identity` and logical-identity absolute-path guards were proven only by a POSIX `/` prefix, so dropping the UNC and drive-letter disjuncts stayed green, and the `session.stopped` **checkpointed** arm was proven only by a null checkpoint, so dropping `!resumable` stayed green. Each now has one isolated negative per disjunct at both identity entries.
- SCOPE, measured and disclosed rather than fixed: the same two derived sweeps were pointed at `canonical.go` and `closed_shapes.go`, the preceding leaves, because "we did not look" and "there is nothing there" are different facts. 262 mutants, 200 killed, 12 not compilable, **50 survivors**. All 18 guard-deletion survivors are equivalent mutants already declared in `unreachableRefusalSites` with a subsuming refusal and a named proving test, matched return-by-return. The other **32 are real one-directional proofs** in the preceding leaves — an equality coupling with a counterpart on one lexical side only, and a strictly-sorted list proven by reversal but never by a duplicate. Both shapes were claimed closed for `core_records.go` in that revision. The full per-survivor list, with what each mutant silently admits, ships as `TASK-260830-1tax26_rev4-preceding-leaf-survivors.md`; the natural owner is the sibling `TASK-260830-uqnwmi`, whose deliverable is exactly this conformance-test class.
- CORRECTION (2026-09-02, revision 6): "Both shapes are closed for `core_records.go` in this revision" does not reproduce and is corrected above. The revision-4 grammar rewrote comparison OPERATORS, which cannot express either of the two shapes review then found in `core_records.go`: a boolean-valued `!=` has no operator to rewrite, only a boolean identity to split into `a && !b` / `!a && b`; and `epoch != 1` narrowed to `epoch > 1` is an equivalent mutant, so the operator sweep provably could not reach the `epoch >= 3` narrowing that admitted an epoch-2 lease with a null predecessor. The honest revision-4 claim is: the one-directional EQUALITY-COUPLING shape the operator grammar could express was closed for `core_records.go`; the boolean-identity and literal-boundary shapes were never swept there. Both are closed in revision 6 by standing derived gates, not by a sweep.
- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/`, `README.md`, `LOGBOOK.md`
- STATUS: Resolved in revision 4.


### 0420 — Canonical decode nesting depth capped at 256
- ROOT CAUSE: `internal/canonicaljson/canonical.go` `decodeValue` recursed once per nesting level with no bound; a 2,000,000-byte document of one million nested arrays exhausted the goroutine stack and killed the process with uncatchable `fatal error: stack overflow` at `Canonicalize`, `CalculateObjectIdentity`, and `VerifyObjectIdentity`, well under the 5,242,880-byte identity size cap.
- FIX: `maxNestingDepth = 256` (`internal/canonicaljson/canonical.go:37`) enforced inside `decodeValue` before it opens a container (`canonical.go:362`), typed `ErrInvalidJSON`; every public entry inherits it through the shared `decodeStrict`.
- DECISION: 256 chosen because the deepest pinned closed shape (16-level submodule tree) decodes at roughly depth 40 and open extensions stop at depth 4, giving more than sixfold headroom; the bound stays far below encoding/json's 10,000-level cap and jcs's unbounded re-parse, so the typed refusal is always the first gate a deep document meets.
- FINDING: the delete-gate mutant reproduces the exact crash (`runtime: goroutine stack exceeds 1000000000-byte limit`, exit 1 with fatal error) through the new regression test; widen-512 and narrow-128 mutants both redden the suite because tests pin the literal 256/257 rather than reading the constant.
- FINDING: registering new evidence tests in `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` requires repinning `reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256` in `internal/traceability/traceability.go:42`; tracecheck fails closed on the projection digest until the repin is reviewed.
- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/canonical.go`, `internal/canonicaljson/nesting_depth_test.go`, `internal/canonicaljson/testdata/constraint-enumeration.md`, `internal/canonicaljson/testdata/fuzz/FuzzCanonicalizeRoundTrip/nesting-depth-over-limit`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json`, `internal/traceability/traceability.go`, `README.md`.
- STATUS: fixed in STORY-260902-3os1kh workspace (trunk 48db30b descendant); canonicaljson coverage 87.1% -> 87.2%; handed off to review.

### 0130 — Coverage is the only honest proof that a gate was reached
- FINDING: `go test ./...` was fully green — seeded fuzz corpora and `tracecheck` included — with **eight normative gates deleted simultaneously** from `internal/canonicaljson/core_records.go`: Safe Boundary `foreground_idle`-true, both `open_*` counters-zero, `accepted_risks` vocabulary and order, workspace member sort/uniqueness, `sanitized_remote_urls` sort/uniqueness, `session.stopped` bootstrap_abort graceful-false, Observation `phase` lower_snake, and Observation stream `+1` continuity. Seven further single-gate narrowings also survived. Package coverage at the time was 87.7%.
- ROOT CAUSE, structural rather than a list of missed cases: `requireExactMembers` runs FIRST in every validator, so every negative fixture that OMITS a member short-circuits on the closed-member sweep and never reaches the type, format or coupling refusal it claims to pin. **91 runtime refusal branches in that one file were never executed once.** The suite proved the closed member set and the declared bounds thoroughly and proved almost nothing about what each member must *contain*.
- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/refusal_guards_test.go` derives every refusal-emitting return in the package's production sources — refusal *constructors* derived rather than named — then runs the shipped suite in a child process under a statement-coverage profile and requires each derived site to have been **executed**. Not a claim in a table: an observed count. A guard added without a negative case, a deleted negative case, an obsolete unreachability claim, and a declaration naming a guard that no longer exists each redden the gate; all four are demonstrated in `TASK-260830-1tax26_rev4-verify-derivation-selfcheck.log`.
- NOTE: the general property. `declared_bounds_test.go` proves a bound's *obligation* is claimed; only coverage proves the gate was *reached*. A derived obligation set answers "is there a case for this?"; a coverage profile answers "did anything run it?". Those are different questions and this leaf needed both.
- FIX: the reach itself is two derived sweeps over every value position of every fixture, so the cases are not hand-listed either — a wrong **JSON type** per member, and a malformed **lexical form** per structurally-shaped value, classified by the production `scalar` parsers. Package coverage 87.7% → 97.1%. Reproduced on the delivered tree: **711 derived refusal sites, 55 declared unreachable with a named subsuming refusal and a named proving test, 656 required executed, 0 unexecuted.**
- DECISION: 55 derived obligations are unreachable through any production entry and are declared with a reason, the refusal that subsumes them, and a test that must exist — never waived. The largest class is the same short-circuit that caused the defect: `requireExactMembers` makes every "identity input requires member X" branch unreachable.
- ANOMALY: a spec-silence trap on the other side. `EnvironmentTuple.environment_version` and `store_schema_fingerprint` are declared **by name only** at the pinned commit, while their siblings carry explicit types, so accepting a number there is correct and demanding a refusal would have invented a constraint — the fourth invented-constraint risk on this Story. Both are declared exemptions quoting the clause, asserted exactly in both directions so an obsolete exemption fails too.
- FINDING: a **deletion-only** sweep is not enough. Narrowing Observation stream continuity from `sequence != previous+1` to `sequence < previous+1` left the suite green: the shipped tests proved only a *repeated* sequence, while a forward **gap** — the normative negative §18.1 names — was silently admitted. A derived narrowing sweep over every comparison operator then showed six identity-equality couplings proven in one lexical direction only, so `a != b` narrowed to a single comparison survived. Both classes closed. The revision-4 sweep is reported in full in `TASK-260830-1tax26_rev4-mutation-sweep.md`; the numbers in this entry are the ones that reproduce from `sweep.py` on the delivered tree, and every survivor is listed rather than summarised.
- CORRECTION: two artifacts overclaimed and are fixed rather than left standing. `TestEverySessionEventClosedVocabularyRefusesComplementAtProductionEntries` said "Every" over a hand-maintained list missing `accepted_risks`; its subject is now derived from the pinned payload inventory (21 hand-listed → 24 derived). The revision-3 LOGBOOK claim that a derived sweep over "all refusal guards" found 8 survivors does not reproduce and has been corrected in place.
- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json`, `LOGBOOK.md`, `README.md`
- STATUS: Resolved; TASK-260830-1tax26 handed to review as revision 4.


## 2026-09-02 — BUG-260902-2faftr: a name blacklist is not secret detection

`hasForbiddenConfigName` refused any `extensions` key, and any nested extension
object key at any depth, containing a `secret`/`token`/`password`/`credential`/
`auth`/`environment`/`env`/`endpoint` part. The pinned v0.5.0 spec states no
such rule: `SPEC.md:345-347` defines an extension key as the reverse-DNS grammar
and reserves no label, and `SPEC.md:347-349` constrains an ExtensionValue object
only to string keys within nesting depth 4, so the nested-key arm had no source
at all.

The finding worth keeping is the failure shape, not the deletion. The rule read
like secret exclusion and was presumably written for `SPEC.md:2596-2597`
("secret, token, endpoint credential ... is forbidden") — but that clause is
scoped to a terminal backend-config `settings` object two sentences earlier, and
the implementation applied it to key *names* in a different section. It
inspected zero values, so `com.example.deploy = "AKIA..."` passed while
`works.relux.env-tools` and `com.example.auth-manager` — namespaces this
organisation owns — were refused. It was pure false-positive surface with no
true-positive capability.

It survived a review loop because `refusal_test.go` pinned it: the loop
hardened the invented constraint instead of asking which spec line declared it.
A refusal test asserts a gate is reachable; it says nothing about whether the
gate should exist. Reviewing a refusal now means quoting its declaring spec line
before accepting its test.

The two clauses that do exist were already enforced where they apply, and both
are now pinned by a test rather than left implicit. `SPEC.md:2596-2597` is
enforced by the closed `BackendSettingsValidator` schema a backend
implementation version registers. `SPEC.md:2562-2563` ("No v2 table accepts a
secret, endpoint credential, model token, auth root, or arbitrary environment
passthrough") — the §6.4 clause an earlier review cited when it demanded
coverage for the blacklist — is enforced by the closed table shape
`decodeStrict` decodes with `DisallowUnknownFields`, which is a field-declaration
rule, not a key-spelling rule. `SPEC.md:2344-2345` draws the same line for
values and goes further: "Secret values MUST NOT be accepted in config fields; a
provider MAY name a machine-local environment variable or credential profile."
The specification explicitly permits a config field to *name* a credential
profile, which is exactly what the removed rule refused.

Same class as BUG-260902-beqfwr (`ssh_args` name blacklist -> derived
admission): where the pinned spec is silent, be permissive; where it names an
equivalence class, derive admission rather than enumerate refusals.

A second finding came out of the review of the reapply, and it is the more
transferable one. README and the commit message both claimed that a nested key
named `endpoint` or `token` is "preserved as data". The tests asserted only that
such a document is *admitted*. Those are different properties: the reviewer
built a mutant that admits the document and then silently deletes every nested
`secret`/`token`/`password`/`credential`/`auth`/`env`/`environment`/`endpoint`
key from the loaded map, and it passed the entire repository suite, `go vet`,
full and scoped `tracecheck` and `cataloggen -check` — reproduced here at exit
0. Nothing in the tree read a nested value back, so nothing could notice.

This is the same shape as the defect the change itself removed, mirrored: there,
a test pinned a rule the specification never declared; here, an artifact
declared a property no test pinned. An admission assertion is evidence about a
gate, never about what survives the gate. The fix is cheap and general — assert
the round trip, not the verdict: `TestExtensionValueObjectKeysArePreservedAsData`
loads each previously blacklisted label through the production
`loadConfigDocument` entry and compares the re-encoded extensions map
byte-for-byte, so a dropped, renamed, retyped or rewritten nested key fails.
`encoding/json` sorts object keys, which is what makes byte identity a usable
assertion here rather than a brittle one.

- PROVENANCE: this change was produced and reviewed to acceptance on BUG-260902-3ru6vw, whose element was discarded when its parent Story was deleted while a move chain still referenced it. The accepted patch was reapplied here, not reimplemented. It did not apply to current trunk unchanged: `BUG-260902-beqfwr` had since landed and removed `parseSSHConfigOption`, so the helper-removal hunk and the README insertion point both had to be relocated by hand. The code and test deltas are byte-identical to the accepted patch.
- STATUS: BUG-260902-2faftr, on `task-board/story/STORY-260902-2oiugz` (forked from trunk `67aed0b`, zero commits behind `main` at measurement time); `internal/config` coverage 94.4%, against a 93.9% pre-change measurement taken from a `git archive HEAD` copy (the preservation tests raise it; no statement lost coverage); `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`, `gofmt -l ./internal`, `go test ./... -count=1`, `go test ./... -cover -count=1`, full and assigned-scope `tracecheck`, and `cataloggen -check` all exit 0; the seven single-clause mutants were re-run on this tree and each exited 1, counting `--- FAIL` subtest lines only (re-added key blacklist reddens 17 of the 20 admission subtests, reproducing the accepted review's measurement exactly; re-added nested-key blacklist 10; dropped grammar gate 11; widened 253-byte key bound 2; widened depth bound 2; dropped registered backend-settings schema call 2; dropped `DisallowUnknownFields` 5), with the tree restored green after each. An eighth mutant closes the review's non-blocking finding: deleting every blacklisted-label nested key from the loaded map passes the whole suite on the previous revision (exit 0) and reddens all 11 preservation subtests here (exit 1). With the preservation tests present the two required narrowing mutants redden more, not less: re-added key blacklist 18, re-added nested-key blacklist 21. Handed off to review. No commit hash is named here: this entry ships in the commit it would describe, so the hash cannot be known when it is written.
## 2026-09-01

### 2340 — A self-consistent value is not a pinned value

- ROOT CAUSE: `ProjectionMetadata.SourceFingerprint` was asserted only against itself across three opens of the same source. A constant is maximally self-consistent, so replacing `projectionSourceFingerprint` with `"sha256:" + hex(make([]byte, 32))` satisfied every existing assertion. The whole fingerprint chain — and the scan ordering feeding it — was reachable, exercised, and unfalsifiable.
- FINDING: The determinism test read digest order back through `Objects()`, which carries its own `ORDER BY storage_class, representation, digest`. The loop that looked like it pinned the production `sort.Slice` was decided by the read query in a different function. This is the round-6/7 sibling-decider shape again, one step further out: the decider is not a preceding line or a sibling term but a re-sort on the read path.
- DECISION: Derive the expected fingerprint *outside the module*. `.temp/TASK-260830-3amrl9/fingerprint-derivation.py` re-implements it in Python and executes no Go production code, so the golden cannot be a copy of what the implementation emits today. It independently reproduced the reviewer's probe value exactly.
- FINDING: The self-checking fixture earned its keep immediately. The table re-hashes each pinned content, and the first draft paired the contents with the wrong digests — caught on the first run, not by inspection.
- ANOMALY: `_defensive=1` in the DSN is honoured by modernc.org/sqlite (`SQLITE_DBCONFIG_DEFENSIVE`) and is the only thing stopping the live connection from rewriting `sqlite_master` — precisely the mutation the drift fixtures perform on raw connections. The word appeared exactly once in the whole change, on that DSN line. Two lines above, `_synchronous` carried a full subsumption note; the parameter that *was* individually observable had none.
- DECISION: A refusal test needs a forward control when the refusal could plausibly be the dependency's default. `TestOpenProjectionConnectionRefusesSchemaRewrites` asserts the same two statements *succeed* on a connection opened without `_defensive`, so the refusal is attributed to this projection's configuration rather than to SQLite baseline behaviour.
- DECISION: Extract the inline `sort.Slice` closure to `sortIndexedObjects` so the comparator can be driven directly with a same-digest pair. The scan can never produce that input — one digest owns one path under `digest_path_v1` — so the `RelativePath` tie-break was untestable while the comparator stayed inline.
- ANOMALY: Deleting the ordering *call* from `scanAuthoritativeBlobs` stays green and legitimately so: `os.ReadDir` returns filename-sorted entries and the shard split makes that traversal already ascending by digest. Genuine subsumption by a dependency's contract rather than by another check in the suite — documented at the call site, with the order itself pinned in both directions on the extracted comparator.
- SCOPE: `internal/localstore/projection_test.go` (3 new cases, self-checking fixture, independent fingerprint derivation), `internal/localstore/projection.go` (`sortIndexedObjects` extraction plus two call-site comments), `README.md`.
- STATUS: Resolved. 8 mutants, 7 red as required, 1 green as a documented subsumption. TASK-260830-3amrl9 rev8, handed to review.

### 2320 — A derived inventory must be derived from the construct, not from a list of construct names
- FINDING: A twelve-mutant sweep on this leaf reported "12/12 killed, 0 survivors" and the review then found roughly twenty declared bounds with no negative test at all. Widening `bounded("terminal_id", 1, 512)` to `1, 4096` — an 8x widening of a declared bound — passed `go test ./...`, both fuzz targets and `tracecheck`. The sweep proved only that the twelve hand-picked gates worked.
- ROOT CAUSE: the same meta-defect as the previous entry, one level up. The gate proved its own coverage over *member sets*; nothing proved coverage over *bounds*, and the sweep that would have caught it selected its own mutants by hand.
- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/declared_bounds_test.go` derives the bound-helper set transitively from the package sources (a function with a bounded `name` and an int `minimum`/`maximum`, plus any wrapper forwarding that name with literal bounds), turns every call site into one obligation per declared direction, and asserts the obligation set equals the proof set in both directions. Because the obligation key carries the literal bound, widening a bound reddens the suite *before any case runs*. 58 call sites across 57 distinct keys, 98 obligation instances.
- DECISION: two obligations are unreachable at a public entry and are declared, not waved through — the 65,536-entry `GitIndex` bound (refused by the outer 5 MiB size gate first) and a 256-entry nested submodule array (refused by the whole-tree submodule count first). Each names its subsuming refusal and a test that must exist in the package, and both sets are asserted exactly.
- FINDING: a mechanically derived sweep over the 135 bound arguments and *entry-level* refusal guards in `core_records.go` found 8 survivors the hand-picked sweep never generated — every refusal `ValidateObservationEvent` and `ValidateObservationStream` emit before the event shape is reached (decode failure, non-AX number, non-object, invalid member event, empty stream) plus the `requireArrayMinimum` type guard. All now covered.
- CORRECTION (2026-09-02, revision 4): the sentence above originally read "all 135 bound arguments and refusal guards in `core_records.go`" and "All now covered". That does not reproduce and overstated the sweep. What that sweep enumerated was every *bound argument* plus the guards whose condition it could rewrite to `false`; it was not the refusal-guard set. A statement-coverage profile of the same tree shows **91 runtime refusal branches in `core_records.go` that no test executed once**, at 87.7% package coverage, and the round-3 review deleted eight normative gates simultaneously with `go test ./...` still fully green. The correct claim for revision 3 is: a derived sweep over bound arguments found 8 survivors among the mutants it generated, and said nothing about the refusal-guard set.
- FINDING: the last survivor is an equivalent mutant, not a gap: `requireArrayMinimum`'s absent-member branch is unreachable because its only call site reads `predecessors` after `requireExactMembers` has already refused an omission. Pinned by name rather than deleted.
- ANOMALY: `Lease Record` carried an invented constraint. Section 5.3 declares `predecessor_lease_id` "Null only at epoch 1", "An epoch-1 `create` lease MUST have a null predecessor", and `checkpoint_id` "Null only for epoch-1 `create`". It nowhere declares that epoch 1 implies `reason = create`, and that inference refused a legitimate epoch-1 `recovery` lease. Removed both inferred directions; the checkpoint rule now correctly covers the whole complement of epoch-1-`create`, which the old epoch-only form missed. Third invented-constraint finding across the m0 leaves.
- NOTE: `validateProviderIdentityRecord` ranged a Go map to apply three bounds, so a record violating several reported a random member — and the bounds were invisible to any call-site derivation. Spelled out as three ordered calls.
- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json`, `README.md`
- STATUS: Resolved; TASK-260830-1tax26 handed to review as revision 3.

### 2300 — Sibling terms hide a clause the same way preceding lines do
- ROOT CAUSE: `equalProjectionSchemaEntry` in `internal/localstore/projection.go` compares four identity fields in one `||` chain. Every existing drift fixture changed the SQL text or the `(type, name)` key, so a sibling term always decided the outcome first and the `actual.table != expected.table` term was reachable, exercised and pinned by nothing. Deleting only that term left the entire `localstore` suite green — reproduced before fixing.
- FINDING: This is the round-5 shape-clause defect in a different syntactic shape. There the check that decided instead of the target was the *next line*; here it is a *sibling term in the same expression*. The isolation rule generalises: a negative fixture must leave every other decider matching, whether that decider sits above the target or beside it.
- FINDING: SQLite accepts `PRAGMA writable_schema=ON; UPDATE sqlite_master SET tbl_name='projection_metadata' WHERE type='index' AND sql IS NULL` and reopens the database. Every other sqlite_master rewrite tried in this package fails as `malformed database schema`. That makes the moved autoindex `tbl_name` the one drift class no other comparison term can observe, and the only fixture that isolates this term.
- DECISION: Extend `readProjectionSchemaShape` with per-field maps (`names`, `tables`, `hasStatement`) rather than reuse its `keys` field. `keys` embeds the table name, so it cannot express "this term changed and those did not" — the assertion the anti-vacuity guard needs.
- DECISION: Verify a reviewer's subsumption argument instead of quoting it. Both `hasStatement` drift directions were re-probed here; the two `SQLITE_CORRUPT` messages reproduce verbatim, but only when the raw reopen is taken *before* production recovery runs — my first probe read the already-rebuilt database and reported a clean open for both.
- ANOMALY: A green mutant is not evidence of subsumption unless the ordering is checked. `Objects()` size bounds survive deletion because `verifyProjectionSchema` runs inside `openProjection` before any `*Projection` is returned; without that ordering the same green result would mean the bound is simply untested.
- SCOPE: `internal/localstore/projection_test.go` (new tbl_name drift case, near-miss `sha256-old` objects-root subtest, extended shape helper), `internal/localstore/projection.go` (comments only — verified against the rev6 patch, 44 raw diff lines, 0 after stripping comment lines), `README.md`.
- STATUS: Resolved. 6 mutants, 3 red as required, 3 green as documented subsumptions. TASK-260830-3amrl9 rev7, handed to review.

### 2245 — Reaching a refusal clause is not pinning it
- ROOT CAUSE: Every fixture for a *shape* clause in `internal/localstore/projection.go` — the checks that refuse a path because it is not the kind of file it must be — was a directory at mode `0700` or a symlink at lstat permission `0777`. Both are refused by the `verifyOwnerFileInfo(info, 0o600)` call on the following line, so the shape clause was reached but never decided anything the suite could observe. Five clauses survived individual disablement; the blob-leaf pair survived being disabled *together*.
- FINDING: A derived refusal inventory can prove a site was reached. It cannot prove the site decided the outcome. Those are different facts, and positive-path-only coverage of a refusal looks identical to real coverage in both the inventory and the mutant count until the fixture is built so that every preceding check passes.
- FINDING: The isolating fixture for this whole family is one thing — a FIFO created at exactly mode `0600`. Not a symlink, owned by the effective user, already owner-only, so only the shape clause can refuse it. It had been written once, for the lock file, and not applied to any sibling.
- ANOMALY: The blob-leaf regularity clause bounds availability, not only safety. `os.Open` and SQLite block indefinitely on a FIFO with no writer, so removing the clause converts a refusal into an unbounded hang inside `OpenProjection`. A negative case that calls the entry point inline would hang the suite instead of reddening it; the case must assert a bounded return. Mutant `m11` reddens after 20.04s at that bound.
- DECISION: Where an isolating fixture exists, pin the clause rather than annotate it `projection-refusal-subsumed`. The shard-kind clause was provably subsumed by `verifyOwnerDirectory` and was still worth pinning with a 0600 FIFO shard, because subsumption is an argument and a fixture is evidence.
- DECISION: `unix.Flock` mutual exclusion was advertised in `README.md` and pinned by nothing — the 6-worker convergence test passes on SQLite's `busy_timeout` alone. flock arbitrates *open file descriptions*, so holding one in-process reproduces a second process exactly; no subprocess needed.
- SCOPE: `internal/localstore/projection_unix_test.go` (8 new cases, bounded-open helper, fixture-isolation assertion), `README.md`. No production source changed — every round-5 finding was an evidence defect, not a behaviour defect.
- STATUS: Resolved. Full sweep of both projection-owned sources: 34 mutants, 34 red, no survivors, including two forward controls on the inventory gate. TASK-260830-3amrl9 rev6, handed to review.

### 2129 — Derived-completeness gate blind to its own refusal class
- ROOT CAUSE: `internal/localstore/projection_refusal_inventory_test.go` derived expected refusal sites from the production AST but matched only `*ast.Ident` calls named `projectionRefusal`. Six live clauses written as bare `fmt.Errorf("%w: ...", ErrUnsafeOwnership)` or as delegated `verifyOwnerFileInfo`/`verifyOwnerDirectory` guards were invisible to the gate whose job was to notice them, and shipped unpinned while 20+ funnelled sites were enforced.
- FINDING: A completeness gate over a *pattern* must also police the pattern. Deriving the expected set is not enough if the extraction rule silently excludes a whole syntactic class.
- FIX: Second funnel `projectionOwnershipRefusal` (`internal/localstore/projection.go:117`) keeps the refusal decision at its call site while preserving the owner-only cause; every projection refusal rerouted through a funnel. Gate now derives two properties — funnel sites need an exercised negative path beneath `openProjection`, AND no projection-owned source may raise a refusal outside a funnel. Build-context filtering added so a platform-specific refusal is not demanded from a platform that cannot run it.
- ANOMALY: I reproduced the identical bug class inside the fix. The new unrouted check sat behind `typed.Fun.(*ast.Ident)`, and `fmt.Errorf` is a `*ast.SelectorExpr`, so the exact class stayed invisible in code written specifically to catch it. First run of mutant m41 exited 0. Reading the new gate would not have found this; only the behaviour-preserving mutant did.
